#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}Clinical Interpretation:{tag2} CCC=0.67 sits in the band this analysis "
"prints as fair\n"
"                            concordance (0.40-0.70). Both components are "
"down here: r=0.82 is scatter around the line, and\n"
"                            C{tag3}b{tag4}=0.82 says the line itself sits "
"off identity. Unlike the previous example, the\n"
"                            disagreement is therefore not a pure offset."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}Clinical Interpretation:{tag2} CCC=0.75 sits in the band this analysis "
"prints as\n"
"                            moderate concordance (0.70-0.90), despite a high "
"correlation (r=0.89). Correlation only asks\n"
"                            whether the two clones rank cases in the same "
"order; CCC also penalises distance from the line of\n"
"                            identity, and C{tag3}b{tag4}=0.84 locates the "
"loss there - a systematic offset, not scatter."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}Clinical Interpretation:{tag2} CCC=0.93 sits in the band this analysis "
"prints as good\n"
"                            concordance (0.90-0.95). CCC multiplies "
"precision (r=0.94, how tightly the paired counts follow a\n"
"                            straight line) by accuracy (C{tag3}b{tag4}=0.99, "
"how close that line lies to the 45-degree line of\n"
"                            identity); both are high here, which is why CCC "
"comes out close to r."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}Clinical Interpretation:{tag2} CCC=0.96 sits in the band this analysis "
"prints as\n"
"                            substantial concordance (0.95-0.99), with both "
"high precision (r=0.97) and high accuracy\n"
"                            (C{tag3}b{tag4}=0.99)."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}Clinical Interpretation:{tag2} Each expert repeated their own "
"dysplasia grades\n"
"                            consistently (intra-rater {tag3} 0.68-0.84). The "
"pooled inter-rater row does not separate baseline\n"
"                            from month 6; running the analysis on the six "
"baseline columns and then on the six month-6 columns\n"
"                            gives one kappa per time point. Two such kappas "
"from the same 80 cases that differ by a few\n"
"                            hundredths are well inside the sampling "
"variation a kappa on 80 cases carries."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}Clinical Interpretation:{tag2} High correlation (r=0.96) masks "
"systematic underestimation\n"
"                            by digital method. CCC (0.88) correctly "
"identifies poor agreement requiring calibration before\n"
"                            clinical use."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}Clinical Interpretation:{tag2} Individual consistency across the shift "
"is substantial to\n"
"                            almost perfect ({tag3} 0.76-0.91). The pooled "
"inter-rater row mixes Hour 0 and Hour 8 reads, so it\n"
"                            cannot show whether group agreement fell over "
"the shift; for that, run the analysis once on the five\n"
"                            Hour 0 columns and once on the five Hour 8 "
"columns and compare the two kappas with their intervals."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}Clinical Interpretation:{tag2} Individual consistency between glass "
"and digital\n"
"                            platforms is good to excellent by the Koo {tag3} "
"Li (2016) bands (ICC 0.78-0.91), and inter-rater\n"
"                            reliability is good (ICC=0.82). An ICC is the "
"share of the total variance that sits between\n"
"                            cases, so a high value does not exclude a "
"systematic offset between platforms: adding a constant\n"
"                            to every WSI count leaves the between-case "
"variance untouched and the ICC barely moves. The\n"
"                            Bland-Altman Statistics table in this analysis "
"is where such an offset shows up, as the Mean\n"
"                            Difference, with the Lower and Upper limits of "
"agreement giving the spread of individual\n"
"                            case differences around it."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}Clinical Interpretation:{tag2} Individual reproducibility is good to "
"excellent\n"
"                            (ICC 0.79-0.92) and inter-rater reliability is "
"good (ICC=0.81). Each intra-rater ICC rests on 60\n"
"                            cases read twice, and its precision is set by "
"that case count, not by how many scorers took part;\n"
"                            all four are point estimates carrying sampling "
"error, so the ordering among scorers is not\n"
"                            established by the point estimates alone."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}Clinical Interpretation:{tag2} On the Landis {tag3} Koch bands the "
"table uses, Resident3's\n"
"                            individual consistency is almost perfect "
"({tag4}=0.81) and Resident2's is moderate ({tag5}=0.58);\n"
"                            Resident2 may need additional training. The "
"pooled inter-rater agreement is substantial ({tag6}=0.64)."
msgstr ""

