#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What each number is:{tag2} how far that resident's grades exceed "
"chance agreement with\n"
"                        one senior pathologist's grades on the same 100 "
"cases. It is agreement with a particular reader, not\n"
"                        a measure of diagnostic ability, and it absorbs any "
"error in the reference grades themselves - a\n"
"                        resident who is right where the senior reader is "
"wrong scores lower, not higher."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What Finn's coefficient reports:{tag2} the share of the score variance "
"that is not rater disagreement, on a case set where nearly every biopsy is "
"graded {tag3}none{tag4}. That describes how these four readers behaved on "
"these cases; it is not evidence that their grades are correct, since four "
"readers can agree on the same wrong grade"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What is computed:{tag2} (U - L){tag3}2{tag4} / (U + L) on 1 df, where "
"U and L\n"
"                        are the total counts above and below the diagonal of "
"the two-rater table\n"
"                        ({tag5}irr::rater.bias{tag6}). This is a "
"McNemar-style test of net direction."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "{tag1}What is it?{tag2}"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What it does not tell you:{tag2} if instead B over-called G2 at A's "
"expense\n"
"                        and A over-called G3 at B's expense, the two "
"directions would cancel and this test\n"
"                        would return p = 1 even though the two pathologists "
"use the grades quite differently.\n"
"                        Enable the {tag3}Bhapkar{tag4} test for that "
"question. This test needs exactly\n"
"                        two raters; with three or more, compare them "
"pairwise or use the rater profile plots."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What it reports:{tag2} the criterion is met or not met at the stated "
"limit and coverage; it does not by itself settle whether one measurement "
"method may take the place of the other in practice"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What it shows:{tag2} The algorithm orders cases similarly to the "
"expert consensus grade; no outcome data enter the statistic, so it says "
"nothing about prognostic performance"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What it shows:{tag2} The four pathologists order tumors by "
"aggressiveness consistently with one another. This is reproducibility, not "
"prognostic validity - no outcome data enter the statistic"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What it shows:{tag2} The pathologists order cases by mitotic activity "
"similarly. Counts that differ by a constant factor still correlate "
"perfectly, so this is not agreement on the count that drives the grade, and "
"no outcome data enter it"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What it shows:{tag2} The pathologists order cases by proliferation "
"similarly. Correlation is unchanged by systematic scaling differences, so it "
"does not show that the reported percentages agree, and no outcome data enter "
"it"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What p = 0.001 shows:{tag2} the two criteria sets produce different "
"category totals on the same 90 biopsies by more than chance. The test "
"compares totals, not individual cases, so it does not report how many "
"biopsies changed category or in which direction any single one moved - the "
"contingency table in this analysis shows that"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What p = 0.03 says:{tag2} the algorithm's and the pathologist's "
"category totals differ by more than chance would explain. The test compares "
"totals only, so it does not say which of the two is closer to the truth on "
"any case - the pathologist's grades are the comparator here, not a verified "
"reference standard"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What p = 0.04 shows:{tag2} the reader's category totals moved between "
"the two readings by more than chance. A shift in totals is not by itself a "
"shift toward the correct diagnosis, and with a single uncontrolled "
"before-and-after reading the test cannot separate the workshop from practice "
"or recall of the cases"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What that does and does not show:{tag2} Marginal homogeneity is the "
"null hypothesis, so failing to reject it does not demonstrate equivalence. "
"The test also compares category totals only and is blind to case-level "
"disagreement, so pair it with a case-level agreement statistic and a "
"prespecified concordance margin before drawing any validation conclusion"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What the coefficient shows:{tag2} The three pathologists order cases "
"by fibrosis severity similarly. Rank correlation says nothing about whether "
"the assigned stages are correct, so it does not on its own support any "
"staging-driven decision"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What the numbers do and do not report:{tag2} the 3.2 mm is an average "
"difference over\n"
"                            200 cases and says nothing about how far a "
"single case can be off - the Bland-Altman limits of\n"
"                            agreement in this analysis carry that spread. "
"Because C{tag3}b{tag4} is not the only shortfall,\n"
"                            subtracting the average offset would leave the "
"two measurements still disagreeing."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What these ICCs cover:{tag2} how consistently each pathologist "
"reproduced their own\n"
"                            mitotic count across the two platforms, and how "
"consistently the four ranked the same cases, in\n"
"                            this one cohort. They carry no information about "
"the size of any glass-to-WSI offset, nor about\n"
"                            behavior at the mitotic-count thresholds that "
"change grade - the Bland-Altman output and\n"
"                            agreement measured at those thresholds are the "
"parts of this analysis that address those."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "{tag1}What this analysis does:{tag2}"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What this comparison can and cannot say:{tag2} it reports how "
"consistently the central\n"
"                            reviewers graded at two time points. It is an "
"uncontrolled before-and-after look with no group\n"
"                            that skipped recalibration, so even a real "
"difference could not be attributed to the\n"
"                            recalibration session rather than to case mix, "
"drift or practice."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What this shows:{tag2} the pathologist's category totals shifted "
"between the two readings by more than chance. With one reader, no comparison "
"group and no repeat reading without the workshop, the test cannot attribute "
"the shift to the workshop, and it does not say whether the new distribution "
"is the more accurate one"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What W shows:{tag2} The four pathologists order the biopsies by "
"inflammation severity consistently with one another. Concordance among "
"raters says nothing about whether their ranking matches the Nancy "
"Histological Index or predicts response to therapy - no outcome data enter "
"the statistic."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What W shows:{tag2} The four pathologists put the cases in a similar "
"order of difficulty. W measures concordance among rankings and nothing else "
"- no learning outcome enters it - so it does not establish that this "
"ordering picks out teachable cases or that a curriculum built on it works."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What W shows:{tag2} The pathologists rank aggressiveness consistently "
"with one another. It does not show that their ranking matches the FNCLCC "
"grade or predicts outcome - no outcome data enter the statistic, and "
"consistent raters can be consistently wrong."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}What W shows:{tag2} The six pathologists rank degree of response "
"consistently with one another; discordant cases are worth reviewing. "
"Reproducibility among raters is a prerequisite for, but not evidence of, "
"validity as a trial endpoint or predictive biomarker - that requires outcome "
"data, which do not enter W"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}When kappa is low{tag2} - the contingency table reveals "
"{tag3}where{tag4} disagreements occur"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}When PSA ≠ NSA:{tag2} the gap mainly reflects prevalence, not rater "
"bias."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"{tag1}When to prefer them:{tag2} bootstrap intervals make no "
"normal-approximation\n"
"                    assumption, which helps for bounded, skewed statistics "
"such as a kappa near 1. They are not\n"
"                    automatically better than the analytical intervals: with "
"few cases or a rare category a resample\n"
"                    can miss a category, and the interval can be too narrow. "
"Read any dropped-resample note under the\n"
"                    table, and report which interval method you used (the CI "
"Method column says)."
msgstr ""

