msgstr ""

#: decisioncombine/results/pairedComparisonTable.description
msgid ""
"Each combination rule against each test it combines, on the same patients: "
"the cases gained and lost, and the change in sensitivity (disease-present "
"cases) and specificity (disease-absent cases) with a 95 percent confidence "
"interval"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"Each correlation uses the cases that <b>both</b> raters in that pair scored "
"(pairwise deletion), so the number of cases behind each one ranges from %1$d "
"to %2$d. Cases counts the cases rated by at least two raters."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"Each count is one independent patient. If a patient contributes several "
"slides, blocks or lesions, count one result per patient or use a method for "
"clustered data; otherwise the confidence intervals can be too narrow."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"Each data row is treated as a separate case. If the same case was measured "
"under both conditions on two rows, the model does not know they are one "
"case, so this compares conditions between cases and is less precise than a "
"within-case comparison would be. Give a case ID variable to link them."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"Each difference is the first-named test minus the second: for 'A vs B', a "
"positive value favors A and a negative value favors B."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"Each factor term is compared with its predictor's reference (first) level: "
"%s. Points for a factor level apply relative to that reference level (0 "
"points)."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"Each factor term is compared with its predictor's reference (first) level: "
"%s. The penalized estimates depend on that choice: with another reference "
"level the coefficients differ, and LASSO or Elastic Net can retain a "
"different set of terms."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"Each interval is a t interval for that row alone, not a simultaneous "
"interval over the table; with many rows, some will exclude 0 by chance."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"Each interval requires at least 20 finite parameter estimates. Across the "
"class or rule proportion and test response probabilities, successful counts "
"range from {minimum} to {maximum} of {requested}. Intervals with fewer than "
"20 are left blank; increase the sample information or resolve fitting "
"failures before reporting uncertainty."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"Each interval, and each sensitivity or specificity p-value, is for a single "
"comparison and is not adjusted for multiplicity, whereas the pairwise "
"p-values in the McNemar table are Holm-Bonferroni adjusted."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"Each latent-class resample uses the full-sample solution and two additional "
"random starts, followed by likelihood refinement. The main fit uses a larger "
"search, so local-optimum uncertainty may remain. Percentile intervals can be "
"too narrow with weak identification, boundaries or small samples; nominal "
"coverage is not guaranteed."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"Each marker is tested separately against AUC = 0.5 using both tails of the "
"normal approximation. Current N and the class ratio use that marker's "
"complete cases. Selecting other markers does not change this estimand. This "
"does not calculate power for a difference between markers; the legacy "
"correlationROCs setting is ignored."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"Each metric is tested separately for every pair of predictors, so with {k} "
"predictors this table reports up to {m} tests. At the 5% level roughly one "
"verdict in twenty is expected to read “significant” by chance alone, so with "
"several pairs on screen treat an isolated significant row as a hypothesis to "
"confirm rather than as a finding. No multiplicity adjustment is applied to "
"these p-values."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "Each model needs a unique name."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"Each one-vs-one pair is oriented by the outcome's level order: the later "
"level of the pair is read as the case. For a nominal (unordered) outcome "
"that order is arbitrary, so the pairwise AUCs depend on how the levels "
"happen to be ordered; order the levels in Data > Setup when the classes have "
"a natural order."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"Each penalized-EM resample starts from the full-sample solution and two "
"standard starting values, followed by penalized-likelihood refinement. The "
"main fit uses a larger search, so local-optimum uncertainty may remain. "
"Percentile intervals can be too narrow with weak identification, boundaries "
"or small samples; nominal coverage is not guaranteed."
msgstr ""

