#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"Every combination that uses it either reduces to a rule on the other tests "
"alone or calls every case positive, or every case negative."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid "Every combination that uses such a test is affected as well."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"Every complete case used for the combination analysis has gold standard "
"\"{level}\", so the combination analysis contains no disease-absent cases. "
"Specificity cannot be estimated; PPV and NPV are fixed at 100% and 0% by the "
"sampling rather than by any test; Accuracy only repeats the sensitivity; and "
"Balanced Accuracy and Youden's J, which combine sensitivity with "
"specificity, cannot be formed. All of these are reported as blank in the "
"combination table. So are LR+, LR- and the diagnostic odds ratio: each of "
"those needs a specificity, and computing one from the continuity correction "
"would report a number that reflects no patient. Diagnostic accuracy "
"assessment requires both diseased and non-diseased cases. Individual-test "
"tables use their own pairwise-complete cases and may still contain both "
"outcomes."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"Every evaluated threshold, including both ends of the range, gets equal "
"weight, so this differs slightly from the Difference in Average Net Benefit "
"in the Exploratory Model Comparison table, which is the area under the curve "
"divided by the range width."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "Every model-based estimate, with confidence intervals."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"Every observed rating is used: a case is kept even when some pathologists "
"did not read it. Cases with fewer than 2 ratings carry no agreement "
"information."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"Every pair of predictors is tested separately, so with {k} predictors this "
"table reports {m} tests. At the 5% level roughly one verdict in twenty is "
"expected to read “significant” by chance alone, so with several pairs on "
"screen treat an isolated significant row as a hypothesis to confirm rather "
"than as a finding. No multiplicity adjustment is applied to these p-values."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"Every predictor produced a single distinct predicted probability, so there "
"is nothing to group into calibration bins. That happens only when a "
"predictor is constant within the analyzed rows: with Prediction scale Score "
"its logistic slope is then not estimable, and every patient receives the "
"sample prevalence as risk."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"Every recorded result of {test} ({variable}) is \"{level}\", the level "
"chosen as positive, so {test} cannot separate disease-present from "
"disease-absent cases in these data: on its own it reads sensitivity 100% and "
"specificity 0% by construction."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"Every row is estimated directly from the observed joint results, not derived "
"from the individual tests' sensitivity and specificity. These figures "
"therefore do NOT require the tests to be conditionally independent given "
"disease status, and they stay valid when the tests share biology, specimen "
"or technology. That is the main difference from a sequential-testing "
"calculator, which must assume independence and warns about it."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"Every row must be a different patient. Several specimens, cores, blocks, "
"lesions or lymph nodes from the same patient are correlated, and every "
"confidence interval here treats the rows as independent, so with such data "
"the intervals are too narrow. Use one result per patient, or a method that "
"accounts for clustering."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"Every selected column is read as another rater of ONE variable, so this is "
"the single-variable iota over your rater columns. For nominal (categorical) "
"ratings it is numerically identical to Conger's exact kappa, which the Exact "
"kappa (3+ raters) option also reports. The multivariate form of iota - "
"several variables scored by the same raters - is not computed by this "
"analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"Every selected column is read as another rater of ONE variable, so this is "
"the single-variable iota over your rater columns. For nominal (categorical) "
"ratings with two raters it is numerically identical to Cohen's kappa, which "
"the main agreement table already reports; the Exact kappa (3+ raters) option "
"needs three or more raters and will stop the analysis here. The multivariate "
"form of iota - several variables scored by the same raters - is not computed "
"by this analysis."
msgstr ""

