msgid "LR+ Quality"
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"LR+, LR- and the diagnostic odds ratio add 0.5 to all four cells only when a "
"cell in the ratio's denominator is zero and its numerator is not (FP for "
"LR+, TN for LR-, FP or FN for the odds ratio), so the ratio stays finite "
"(for the odds ratio this is the Haldane-Anscombe correction; the likelihood "
"ratios use the same adjustment). When the numerator cell is zero instead (TP "
"for LR+, FN for LR-, TP or TN for the odds ratio), the ratio is exactly 0 "
"and is not corrected: adding 0.5 there would turn a test that never detected "
"disease into one that seems to point towards it. Its confidence interval "
"runs from 0 to the upper limit of the same log-scale interval computed with "
"0.5 added to every cell. A corrected ratio stands in for an infinite one, so "
"when the correction would bring it to 1 or below (an empty cell in a much "
"smaller reference group) it is left blank as not estimable. The proportions "
"on the same row use the observed counts, so the two need not agree exactly "
"at a zero cell. The correction repairs a sampling zero, not a structural "
"one. On a row with no test-positive patients (a pattern nobody shows, or a "
"rule that is never positive), LR+ and the odds ratio are 0/0 and are left "
"BLANK rather than corrected, while LR- is exactly 1: any other result leaves "
"the odds unchanged. On a row with no test-negative patients, LR- and the "
"odds ratio are blank and LR+ is exactly 1. In a sample with only one "
"reference outcome all three are blank in every row, as are PPV and NPV, "
"which the sampling alone fixes, and Accuracy, which then only repeats the "
"sensitivity or specificity."
msgstr ""

