msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The '%1$s' metric is best at its %2$s, so the cut-point was found by %3$s "
"it; the Cutpoint method setting asked for the opposite direction."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The '%1$s' preset replaced settings you can still see in the options panel: "
"%2$s. Choose the Custom Configuration preset if you want the values you "
"entered to be used."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The 'Logistic' rows are unpenalized refits (shown for AIC comparability); "
"their apparent AUC is typically higher than the penalized row because "
"refitting removes shrinkage. All metrics are apparent (in-sample) - use "
"Bootstrap Internal Validation for optimism-corrected discrimination."
msgstr ""

#: R/sequentialtests.b.R
msgid ""
"The \"{preset}\" teaching example supplies round, illustrative numbers "
"chosen to demonstrate how sequential testing behaves. They are NOT validated "
"clinical parameters, they are not taken from any specific published study, "
"and the prevalence is not your population's. This example is not clinical "
"guidance and must not be used to design a real testing protocol or advise a "
"patient. Replace every value with sensitivity, specificity and prevalence "
"estimates from your own setting or from a source you have checked."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The {ratio} has an empty denominator cell, so its observed value is "
"infinite. Adding 0.5 to every cell would put it at or below 1 and reverse "
"its direction, because the empty cell lies in the smaller reference group, "
"so it is left blank."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The {tag1}multivariate{tag2} form of iota - several variables, say tumor "
"size,\n"
"                            grade and mitotic count, scored by the same "
"raters and summarized in one coefficient -\n"
"                            is {tag3}not computed by this analysis{tag4}. "
"Nothing in this dialog can say which\n"
"                            columns belong to which variable. Entering the "
"columns of several variables side by\n"
"                            side does {tag5}not{tag6} produce the "
"multivariate coefficient: it produces the\n"
"                            single-variable iota of a table whose "
"{tag7}raters{tag8} are really different\n"
"                            measurements, and that number does not describe "
"your study. For the multivariate\n"
"                            form, call {tag9}irr::iota(){tag10} in R with "
"one matrix per variable."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The %.0f%% interval is an <i>approximation</i>: a Wilson interval applied to "
"specific agreement, which is not a binomial proportion, on the count of "
"cases rated into the category by at least one rater. Actual coverage is not "
"guaranteed, and the interval is least trustworthy for rare categories and "
"for values near 0 or 1. de Vet and colleagues (2013) recommend a bootstrap "
"interval instead; treat these limits as indicative."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The %1$s analyzed rows that carry a patient identifier (%2$s) come from %3$s "
"patients, so some patients contribute more than one row (slides, cores or "
"lesions). Every panel except the Frozen-model validation report assumes that "
"each row is an independent patient. Rows from one patient are correlated, so "
"the AUC confidence intervals, the tests comparing markers and the confidence "
"intervals for sensitivity and specificity are too narrow, and p-values too "
"small. • Analyze one row per patient (for example a patient-level score), or "
"• for a fixed model with a prespecified cut-off, enable the Frozen-model "
"validation report, whose intervals resample whole patients (every row of a "
"patient must then share one outcome)."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The 95 percent confidence interval for the accuracy difference comes from "
"Newcombe's method 10, a different procedure from McNemar's test, and it "
"excludes 0, the value of no difference; the two procedures disagree on these "
"data, so report both rather than either alone."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The 95% confidence interval beside Youden's J is the Agresti-Caffo interval "
"for a difference of two independent proportions, sensitivity minus the "
"false-positive rate. No row is adjusted for multiple comparisons or for "
"choosing among rules: treat the best-looking row as a hypothesis to confirm "
"in new patients."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"The 95% confidence interval for Youden's index includes 0, so this sample "
"cannot tell the test apart from chance, whichever side of 0 the estimate "
"falls. Report it as showing no evidence of discrimination, not as poor or as "
"worse than chance. If you expected the test to work, check the levels chosen "
"under Test positive level and Disease present level, and whether the sample "
"gives the precision you need."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"The 95% confidence intervals are calculated from your study sample. They do "
"NOT apply to the PPV and NPV in the main table, which are recomputed by "
"Bayes' theorem at the population prevalence you supplied."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The achieved value is the rate in this sample at the selected cutpoint. It "
"is not guaranteed in another sample. Cutpoint selection and performance "
"assessment use the same observations, so these are apparent estimates "
"requiring independent validation."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The agreement-based metric counts exact equality only, and these ratings are "
"continuous. A pair of cases received exactly the same value from {pct} "
"percent of the raters on average, and every near miss counts as complete "
"disagreement."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The agreement-based metric counts exact equality only, and these ratings are "
"continuous. A pair of raters recorded exactly the same value on {pct} "
"percent of the cases on average, and every near miss counts as complete "
"disagreement."
msgstr ""

#: kappaSizePower/options/kappa1.description.ui
msgid ""
"The alternative hypothesis value of kappa - the level of agreement the study "
"should be able to detect against kappa0."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"The alternative kappa ({kappa1}) is BELOW the null ({kappa0}). This sizes a "
"study to demonstrate that agreement is WORSE than the null value, not "
"better. If you meant to detect an improvement, swap the two values - the "
"required sample size is not the same either way."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"The analysed cases contain {events} patients with the outcome and "
"{nonevents} without it, an exact {ratio} ratio of patients with the outcome "
"to patients without it, as a case-control design that samples a fixed number "
"of controls per case (or of cases per control) would produce."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "The analysis automatically selects appropriate statistics based on data type:"
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"The analysis continues without confidence intervals; the point estimates are "
"unaffected."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The analysis stopped before the combination table was computed; the error "
"above says why."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "The analysis was stopped to avoid reversing event and non-event status."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "The analysis was stopped to avoid reversing intervention recommendations."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"The analysis will show whether using your prediction model(s) provides more "
"clinical benefit than treating all patients or treating no patients."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The annotators tied on %1$d of %2$d cases (no single most-frequent label). "
"%3$d of those could not be resolved under the selected tie-breaking rule and "
"are excluded from the strict-consensus figures; the remainder were resolved "
"by that rule. Strict agreement is sensitive to this choice when ties are "
"common."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The association (weighted) kappa of Nelson and Edwards is a function of the "
"latent ICC alone, so it adds nothing to it and is not shown."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The asymptotic McNemar test without continuity correction comparing %1$s and "
"%2$s did not detect a significant difference in diagnostic accuracy (n = "
"%3$d paired cases; χ²(%4$d) = %5$.2f, p %6$s)."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The asymptotic McNemar test without continuity correction comparing %1$s and "
"%2$s showed a significant difference in diagnostic accuracy (n = %3$d paired "
"cases; χ²(%4$d) = %5$.2f, p %6$s)."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The AUC in Context column restates it as a percentage, and the AUC "
"Interpretation column places the AUC as displayed in a band that includes "
"its lower edge: Excellent from 0.9, Good from 0.8, Fair from 0.7, Poor from "
"0.6, Minimal from 0.5, Below chance under 0.5."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "The AUC is computed over all cutpoints and is not affected by that search."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The AUC is the probability that a randomly chosen case has a HIGHER value "
"than a randomly chosen non-case (ties count one half)."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The AUC is the probability that a randomly chosen case has a LOWER value "
"than a randomly chosen non-case (ties count one half)."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The AUC of %1$s was %2$.3f (%3$s%% CI: %4$s--%5$s); the interval includes "
"0.5, so these data are compatible with no discrimination."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid "The AUC of %1$s was %2$.3f (%3$s%% CI: %4$s--%5$s)."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid "The AUC of %1$s was %2$.3f (interval not estimable)."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"the balance between sensitivity and specificity, by taking the cutpoint at "
"which the two come closest to being equal; the Optimization Metric setting "
"is not used by this method"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The BCa acceleration uses a random subsample of %1$d of the %2$d cases for "
"the leave-one-out step (drawn from the same seed), and is then rescaled by "
"the square root of the ratio of leave-one-out replicates used to the %2$d "
"cases. Without that rescaling the acceleration comes out about "
"sqrt(%2$d/%1$d) times too large - each leave-one-out deviation is set by the "
"full case count while the sums run over the subsample only - and both "
"interval endpoints are shifted too far."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"the best AUC is {auc}, below 0.5 - Positive class may name the wrong level, "
"Direction may not match the marker, or the marker may not discriminate at all"
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid "The best performing predictor was '%1$s' with an AUC of %2$.3f.%3$s"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The Bhapkar test is a {tag1}chi-square test for marginal homogeneity{tag2} "
"between\n"
"                        {tag3}two raters{tag4} with {tag5}multiple "
"categories{tag6}. It tests whether two\n"
"                        raters use rating categories with equal frequency. "
"This is the extension of {tag7}McNemar's test{tag8}\n"
"                        (which is limited to 2{tag9}2 tables) to larger "
"contingency tables."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The bias grows as the sample gets smaller and as more candidate cutpoints "
"are searched."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The Bland-Altman method assesses {tag1}agreement between two continuous "
"measurements{tag2}\n"
"                        by plotting the difference between methods against "
"their mean. It is the gold standard for\n"
"                        method comparison studies in clinical research."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The bootstrap confidence band could not be computed for %s, so the ROC curve "
"is drawn without it."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The bootstrap confidence limits refer to the raw partial AUC, not the "
"normalized partial AUC."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The bound is a confidence limit around the anticipated kappa and approaches "
"it from below without ever reaching it, so while the anticipated kappa is "
"itself 0.40 or below no sample size, number of raters or significance level "
"can lift the bound past 0.40; the anticipated agreement, not the number of "
"subjects, is what binds here."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The bound is the lower root of the goodness-of-fit chi-square equation, "
"solved directly; a positive bound is shown rounded down (to three decimals, "
"or to two significant digits below 0.001), so the figure shown never "
"overstates it."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The bound shown is the lower of two: the one obtained holding the category "
"proportions at their anticipated values (the published calculation, and "
"kappaSize's) and the one obtained re-estimating them by maximum likelihood "
"at each value of kappa (the interval that keeps its stated coverage)."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The Brier score is the mean squared difference between the predicted risk "
"and the outcome."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The calculation assumes both tests are performed in parallel and allows "
"conditional dependence (correlation %1$s among diseased and %2$s among "
"non-diseased subjects)%3$s."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The calculation assumes both tests are performed in parallel and are "
"conditionally independent."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The calibration slope comes from a logistic regression of the outcome on the "
"logit of the predicted risk, and the Intercept (slope model) column is that "
"model's intercept; calibration-in-the-large is the intercept with the slope "
"fixed at 1, which Van Calster et al. (2019) call the calibration intercept."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The calibration slope is not estimable: every case received the same "
"predicted probability, so there is no linear predictor to calibrate."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The calibration slope is not estimable: the predicted probabilities separate "
"the two outcome classes completely on these data, so the slope is infinite "
"rather than a number. Treat the apparent performance as an artefact of "
"separation."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The calibration-slope optimism rests on %1$d of the %2$d completed "
"replicates: in the others the replicate model separated the outcome classes "
"or gave every case the same probability, so it had no finite slope. "
"Separation is most likely in the replicates that overfit most, so this "
"correction may be too small."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The candidate-rule ranking is a descriptive argmax over observed Youden's J, "
"taken over every row of the table -- result patterns, single tests and "
"strategies -- with no significance test and no multiplicity correction. On a "
"tie the rule using fewer tests is shown first. On data with no real signal "
"it will still name a winner. It is a hypothesis to confirm in new data, not "
"a validated recommendation. Tick \"Youden's J corrected for rule selection\" "
"for a bootstrap estimate of how much of the highest J is the luck of being "
"picked from many rules."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The Case column is the row number in your data, so a case flagged here can "
"be located in the spreadsheet even when a filter is active."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The cases' mean scores differ considerably between institutions, which "
"reflects case mix or site-level scoring; this design cannot separate the two."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "The cases' mean scores differ little between institutions."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The cases' mean scores differ moderately between institutions, which "
"reflects case mix or site-level scoring; this design cannot separate the two."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The category labels are not numbers, so interval and ratio alpha scored the "
"categories 1, 2, 3 and so on in their declared order and read the distances "
"off those positions. If the categories are not equally spaced, choose the "
"Ordinal data type, which uses only their order."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"The chance of a negative result in patients with the disease divided by the "
"chance in patients without it. <0.1 is strong evidence AGAINST disease, "
"0.1-0.2 moderate, 0.2-0.5 weak."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The chi-square test evaluates whether the observed agreement (W) is "
"statistically different from\n"
"                        random chance. A significant p-value (< 0.05) "
"indicates that raters agree more than expected by chance.\n"
"                        However, even statistically significant agreement "
"may not be clinically meaningful if W is low."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The class variable %1$s has fewer than two observed levels; ROC analysis "
"needs cases with and without the condition."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid "The Clinical Application Metrics table reports the PPV at %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"The clinical decision rule label \"{label}\" duplicates a model or "
"reference-strategy name."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "The clinical decision rule variable must be binary (exactly 2 levels)."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The Clopper-Pearson intervals for sensitivity and specificity in the "
"Diagnostic Performance table treat this cutpoint as fixed in advance, so "
"they are centered on these optimistic values and understate the uncertainty."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"the coefficient fell outside the -1 to 1 parameter space and has been "
"clamped to the nearest bound"
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid "The Coefficient Plot is empty because no term was retained."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The combination table therefore contains no case with {test} negative: the "
"\"{alone}\" row reads sensitivity 100% and specificity 0% by construction, "
"every pattern with {test} negative is empty, and the other rows, the ranking "
"included, describe the tests only among cases with {test} positive."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The combination table therefore contains no case with {test} positive: the "
"\"{alone}\" row reads sensitivity 0% and specificity 100% by construction, "
"every pattern with {test} positive is empty, and the other rows, the ranking "
"included, describe the tests only among cases with {test} negative."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The combination table therefore contains only cases where {first} and "
"{second} agreed: every pattern in which they disagree is empty, and every "
"row describes the tests in those cases only."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The combination table therefore contains only cases where {first} and "
"{second} disagreed: every pattern in which they agree is empty, and every "
"row describes the tests in those cases only."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The combination table therefore contains only cases where the tests agreed: "
"every pattern in which they disagree is empty, and every row describes the "
"tests in those cases only."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The combination table therefore contains only cases where the tests "
"disagreed: every pattern in which they agree is empty, and every row "
"describes the tests in those cases only."
msgstr ""

#: R/sequentialtests.b.R
msgid ""
"the combined sensitivity above is too high and the combined specificity too "
"low"
msgstr ""

#: R/sequentialtests.b.R
msgid ""
"the combined specificity above is too high and the combined sensitivity too "
"low"
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid "The complete cases include %1$d events and %2$d non-events."
msgstr ""

