"the confidence interval came back inverted, with the lower bound above the "
"upper bound, and has been suppressed"
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The confidence interval includes 0.5, so these data are compatible with no "
"discrimination."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The confidence interval is left blank for {n} row(s) ({patterns}) whose "
"ratio is determinate but whose standard error is not estimable: the row has "
"an empty test margin (no patient, or every patient, is positive under it), "
"so the delta-method standard error on the log scale is exactly zero and an "
"interval computed from it would be zero-width. A zero-width interval would "
"assert that the ratio is known exactly on a row carrying no information, so "
"the point estimate is shown without one."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The confidence interval rows of the table above put that range at %1$.3f to "
"%2$.3f for these data."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The corrected AUC (%1$.3f) is below 0.500. This is a real result, not an "
"error: the bootstrap replicates fit models that pick up chance associations, "
"so the optimism (%2$.3f) exceeds what the model actually achieves. Read it "
"as evidence that these predictors carry no usable signal at this sample size "
"- the model performs no better than, and by this estimate slightly worse "
"than, guessing."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The corrected AUC (%1$.3f) is in the poor band (below 0.7), so read the "
"corrected calibration slope (%2$.3f) with caution: with this little "
"discrimination it is unstable and may reflect chance associations rather "
"than how far the penalty shrank the predictions. The bootstrap estimates the "
"average optimism of the whole selection procedure, so when chance "
"associations were selected in this dataset it can understate this model's "
"optimism, and the corrected AUC may also be too high."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The correction shown is the optimism of a logistic regression of the outcome "
"on each marker, fitted to these data. With one marker the only part of that "
"model that changes the AUC is its direction (the sign of the slope), which "
"these data choose. For a clearly discriminating marker the optimism is "
"negligible, but for a weak marker in a small sample it can be several AUC "
"points: about 0.07 for a marker carrying no information with 20 positive and "
"20 negative patients."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "the criterion belonging to the cutpoint method you selected"
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The CROC AUC weights the ROC curve toward its low false-positive end with an "
"exponential magnifier of strength alpha (Swamidass et al. 2010). It is never "
"larger than the ROC AUC, so read it against the random-ranking value, "
"1/alpha - 1/(exp(alpha) - 1), not against the ROC AUC, and report alpha with "
"the area. Gain over random = CROC AUC - random-ranking CROC AUC. Tied scores "
"are integrated along the straight ROC segment that joins their operating "
"points."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "The current outcome \"{var}\" has {n} levels: {levels}."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The current row and the two scenarios have {current}, {scenario1} and "
"{scenario2} reference-positive/reference-negative cases. Moving a cut-off on "
"one cohort changes neither count, so these rows cannot be interpreted as "
"thresholds applied to the same participants."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "The current rule variable \"{var}\" has {n} levels: {levels}."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"The custom model-name list must contain exactly {n} non-empty "
"comma-separated names."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The cut point for %s left no case on one side (nobody above it, or "
"everybody), so it could separate no one and its points would rest on no "
"contrast. It was moved to the sample median, or to just below the highest "
"value when ties make the median the maximum. The criterion column shows the "
"cut actually applied."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid "the cut points you supplied"
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"the cut points you supplied, and the sample median for the predictors you "
"did not give one for"
msgstr ""

