"The cut-off comparison is omitted because these scenarios have invalid "
"frequencies: {scenarios}. Their rows are left blank; the other rows and the "
"rest of the analysis are unaffected."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The cut-point was chosen on these same data, so the sensitivity, specificity "
"and accuracy reported at it are optimistic."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid "The cutoff is infinite, so everyone is classified the same way."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The cutpoint was chosen to optimize %s on these same data. The sensitivity, "
"specificity and other metrics reported at that cutpoint are therefore "
"optimistically biased and will typically be lower in a new sample; the bias "
"grows as the sample gets smaller and as more candidate cutpoints are "
"searched. Use bootstrap or split-sample validation before quoting these "
"figures."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The cutpoint was supplied by you rather than searched for in these data, so "
"the sensitivity and specificity reported at it are not inflated by cutpoint "
"selection. They remain subject to ordinary sampling error."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "The data contain no rows. Check the dataset and any active filters."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid "The data did not pass clinical validation. • %s"
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid "The dataset contains no rows to analyze."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The decision-space plot places each rule by its sensitivity and specificity, "
"and no rule has both: every complete case has the same reference-standard "
"result, so one of the two cannot be estimated. The notice about the "
"reference standard says which."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The denominator cell of this ratio is zero, so 0.5 was added to every cell "
"(Haldane-Anscombe correction). Its confidence interval uses the same "
"corrected counts."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The dependence parameter for the diseased group (%1$.2f) is not attainable "
"with these sensitivities, so the joint positive probability was truncated "
"from %2$.4f to its bound %3$.4f. The model actually fitted is therefore not "
"the one you specified, and at least one combination of results becomes "
"impossible in that group. Move the value toward 0 until this note "
"disappears; the realized correlation is reported in the Test Dependence "
"panel."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The dependence parameter for the non-diseased group (%1$.2f) is not "
"attainable with these specificities, so the joint false-positive probability "
"was truncated from %2$.4f to its bound %3$.4f. The model actually fitted is "
"therefore not the one you specified, and at least one combination of results "
"becomes impossible in that group. Move the value toward 0 until this note "
"disappears; the realized correlation is reported in the Test Dependence "
"panel."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"The design needs {n} subjects; few agreement studies can enrol this many. "
"Moving kappa1 closer to kappa0 from the same side, or raising the power "
"target, always inflates the sample size, and a rare finding usually does too "
"(for a binary outcome, a prevalence far from 0.5; with more categories, very "
"unequal category proportions) - revisit whichever of these is not a firm "
"requirement."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid "The detailed clinical interpretation could not be generated."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The diagnostic odds ratio is infinite (a zero cell in the 2x2 table), so it "
"cannot be quoted as a number."
msgstr ""

#: decision/options/newtest.description.ui
msgid ""
"The diagnostic test being evaluated for performance. This is typically a "
"new, faster, or less expensive test compared to the gold standard."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The direction of each marker (whether higher or lower values indicate the "
"positive class) was chosen from these same data by comparing the two groups' "
"medians, which biases the AUC upward."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The direction was read from these data — by comparing the two groups' median "
"values — rather than assumed in advance. Because the same data then supply "
"the AUC, the AUC is biased upward, and a marker pointing the wrong way is "
"reported as though it pointed the right way."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The directional discordance test asks whether, on the cases where two raters "
"disagree, the\n"
"                        disagreements run {tag1}one way more often than the "
"other{tag2} - whether one\n"
"                        rater is consistently the higher scorer. It compares "
"the total number of\n"
"                        {tag3}rater 1 higher{tag4} cases with the total "
"number of {tag5}rater 2 higher{tag6}\n"
"                        cases in a single chi-square on 1 degree of freedom. "
"It needs exactly two raters and\n"
"                        both variables must be declared Ordinal with the "
"same clinical scale order."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The directional discordance test requires an ordered categorical scale. At "
"least one selected variable is a continuous measurement, so there are no "
"rating categories to be discordant on. Use Bland-Altman limits of agreement "
"for systematic bias between measurements. If these numbers are category "
"codes rather than measurements, set the variables to Ordinal - or wrap them "
"in factor(..., ordered = TRUE) when calling from R - and the test will be "
"computed."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The directional discordance test requires exactly 2 raters. You have %d. For "
"3+ raters, use pairwise analyses or rater profile plots."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The directional discordance test was not run because both rater variables "
"must be declared Ordinal with the same clinical scale order. For nominal "
"categories use Bhapkar or Stuart-Maxwell to compare marginal distributions."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The directional discordance test was not run because the two ordinal "
"variables do not declare exactly the same category levels in the same order. "
"Harmonize their clinical scale definitions before comparing higher versus "
"lower ratings."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The displayed ROC curve is smoothed (%s), but every reported statistic — "
"AUC, confidence intervals, optimal cut-points and all "
"sensitivity/specificity values — is computed from the empirical curve. A "
"smoothed curve carries no thresholds, so it cannot produce a cut-point."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The enhanced DeLong comparison failed (%s), so a fallback implementation was "
"used; the AUC differences and p-values below come from that fallback."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"The epiR package could not produce intervals for this table, so the two "
"confidence-interval tables are empty."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The epiR package is required for confidence intervals. Install it with "
"install.packages(\"epiR\"), or disable the \"95% CI\" option."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The epiR package is required for the intervals of sensitivity, specificity "
"and the other proportions. Install it or disable confidence intervals. That "
"interval table is left blank; the likelihood-ratio, odds-ratio and Youden's "
"index intervals and every other result on this page are unaffected."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"The epiR package returned no interval estimates for this table, so the two "
"confidence-interval tables are empty."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The EPV threshold of 10 comes from Peduzzi et al. (1996); the other bands "
"are conventions. Rules of thumb like these should give way to sample-size "
"criteria for prediction models (Riley et al. 2019, 2020), and penalization "
"does not make a small sample reliable (Riley et al. 2021)."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid "The estimation did not converge"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The exact agreement and pairwise kappa could not be computed for these "
"ratings."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The expected lower bound could not be computed for this design: it lies too "
"close to the lowest agreement the model allows for these proportions to be "
"resolved numerically, as happens when a category is extremely rare. Use a "
"less extreme prevalence, increase N, or raise the significance level."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid "The expected lower bound for kappa is {bound}."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The expected lower bound is {bound}, at or below zero, so even if the study "
"observes the anticipated kappa it will not be able to rule out agreement no "
"better than chance. Enrol more subjects, add raters, or relax the "
"significance level."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The expected lower bound is {bound}, which on the Landis and Koch scale is "
"no better than fair agreement, so these subjects cannot establish moderate "
"or better reliability even if the raters perform exactly as anticipated. The "
"goodness-of-fit cells are too sparse at this sample size (see Sparse "
"categories) for the bound to be relied on, so it does not show that "
"agreement exceeds chance either."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The expected lower bound is {bound}. The study excludes agreement below that "
"value but leaves everything above it open, and on the Landis and Koch scale "
"a bound under 0.40 is no better than fair agreement. These subjects can "
"therefore show that agreement exceeds chance, but cannot establish moderate "
"or better reliability even if the raters perform exactly as anticipated."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The exploratory difference-mean slope could not be computed (%s), so its "
"p-value cell is empty. An empty cell is NOT evidence that a "
"magnitude-related pattern is absent; inspect the Bland-Altman plot."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The exploratory difference-mean slope has no finite p-value because the "
"differences lie exactly on a line through the pair means, leaving no "
"residual variation to test against, so its p-value cell is empty. Inspect "
"the Bland-Altman plot."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The exploratory difference-mean slope was not tested because the paired "
"differences are constant: every pair differs by the same amount, so there is "
"no magnitude-related pattern to test, and its p-value cell is empty."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"the F statistic came back infinite or undefined although the ratings do "
"differ, so F and its p value have been suppressed"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"the F statistic came back negative, which a ratio of mean squares cannot be, "
"so F and its p value have been suppressed"
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The Fagan nomogram was not drawn because one of the combined likelihood "
"ratios is not a finite number with these parameters: a combination of "
"results is impossible in one disease group because the dependence is at, or "
"was truncated to, the limit the entered values allow (see the notice above). "
"Move the dependence toward 0."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The Fagan nomogram was not drawn because the negative-rule likelihood ratio "
"is zero, which cannot be placed on the nomogram's logarithmic axis: two "
"negative results are impossible in subjects with the disease because the "
"dependence is at, or was truncated to, the limit the entered values allow "
"(see the notice above). Move the dependence toward 0."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The Fagan nomogram was not drawn because the positive-rule likelihood ratio "
"is %.2f, which is below 1. A Fagan nomogram assumes a positive result raises "
"the probability of disease; here it lowers it, which means the tests as "
"specified perform worse than chance. Check the sensitivity and specificity "
"values you entered."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The Fagan nomogram was not drawn because the two tests together carry no "
"information: the positive-rule and negative-rule likelihood ratios are both "
"%.2f, so neither a positive nor a negative combined result changes the "
"probability of disease. Each test is sitting on the chance diagonal "
"(sensitivity = 1 - specificity). Check the sensitivity and specificity "
"values you entered."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"The false-positive penalty in net benefit and the treat-all reference rest "
"on these patients, so the curves and any confidence intervals are imprecise."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The Finn coefficient is a {tag1}variance-based agreement measure{tag2} for "
"categorical data.\n"
"                        Unlike kappa-based measures that focus on exact "
"agreement, Finn's approach uses analysis of variance\n"
"                        to quantify interrater reliability. It is "
"{tag3}especially useful when variance between raters\n"
"                        is low{tag4} (i.e., when agreement is already high)."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The Finn coefficient is at most 1, and it can be negative: it is 1 minus the "
"within-case variance divided by the variance of a uniform random rating on "
"the s-point scale, so a value below 0 means the raters disagree more than "
"raters picking categories at random would."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The fitted classes have equal average selected-positive probabilities within "
"a numerical tolerance of 0.000001. There is no unique high-positive class "
"under this naming rule. Class proportion, response probabilities, predictive "
"values and their intervals are left blank. Observed agreement and available "
"model-fit diagnostics are retained. This tolerance is a numerical safeguard, "
"not a statistical test of class separation."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The fitted high-positive class contains {proportion}% of analyzed cases. "
"This is a diagnostic warning, not a clinical threshold: class orientation is "
"uncertain and predictive values and response-probability estimates can be "
"unstable near the boundary."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "The fitted risk probabilities are unavailable."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The fixed-prior solution does not assign the higher average "
"selected-positive probability to the positive class. Relabeling would change "
"the asymmetric prior objective. Latent estimates and intervals are left "
"blank; reconsider the selected positive levels or use the unpenalized model."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The following predictor variable(s) are not numeric: %s. ROC analysis "
"requires continuous or ordinal numeric predictors, such as biomarker levels, "
"test scores or measurements. • If the variable is ORDINAL, recode it to "
"numbers in rank order (for example Low=1, Medium=2, High=3). • Do not number "
"a NOMINAL variable such as tumor site or histological subtype: the AUC would "
"then be decided by the order you happened to assign rather than by the "
"marker. • For a nominal predictor use contingency table analysis instead."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The forest plot is not drawn for \"{statistic}\" because this analysis does "
"not calculate a confidence interval for that statistic. The bar chart and "
"heatmap can still display it."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The forest plot is not drawn for \"{statistic}\": it runs from -1 to 1, "
"unlike the proportions and ratios on the forest plot axes. Its 95% "
"confidence interval is in the combination table, and the bar chart and "
"heatmap can still display it."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The full model with a rater random effect could not be fitted (%s), so a "
"reduced model (case + cluster) was fitted instead. The rater variance was "
"not estimated: it is absorbed into the residual variance, and its row in the "
"variance decomposition is left empty."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The full model with a rater random effect could not be fitted (%s), so a "
"reduced model without it was fitted instead. The rater variance was not "
"estimated: it is absorbed into the residual variance, and its row in the "
"variance components is left empty."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The full-sample latent fit is not reportable, so bootstrap refitting was "
"skipped. Resolve the convergence, identification or class-orientation "
"warning first."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The generated scoring system gives a single factor %d points, more than the "
"20 the Max-scaled method allows. Switch to the Max-scaled method before "
"using this score at the bedside."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The global Cochran's Q test could not be computed for these data; the reason "
"is given in the Important Information panel. Report the pairwise comparisons "
"and their confidence intervals instead."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The global Cochran's Q test could not be computed for these data; the reason "
"is given in the Important Information panel. The pairwise comparisons and "
"their confidence intervals in the tables above are unaffected."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The global test across all markers could not be computed (%s); its statistic "
"and p-value are left blank."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid "The gold standard and the test are the same variable"
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"The gold standard has more than one level besides \"{pos}\" ({lvls}), so the "
"disease-absent level cannot be inferred. Select it under Reference negative "
"level. Levels you do not name are excluded from the analysis rather than "
"counted as disease-absent."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid "The gold standard has only one level among the analyzed cases"
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The gold-standard variable cannot also occupy a test slot. Select a "
"different variable for each test."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"The goodness-of-fit test also tests that model: if the raters follow another "
"model with the same kappa, it can reject a true kappa0 far more often than "
"the stated significance level, whereas Fleiss' kappa with a jackknife or "
"bootstrap interval that resamples subjects is not tied to the model."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The grading tendency needs ordered categories (a higher and a lower grade); "
"these ratings are nominal, so it is not computed. If the categories have a "
"natural order, set the rater variables to Ordinal."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The grading tendency needs ordered categories (a higher and a lower grade); "
"these two categories are unordered, so it is not computed. If one category "
"is the higher one (for example positive over negative), set the rater "
"variables to Ordinal."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The grading tendency places every pathologist on one common category order, "
"and the selected rater variables do not determine one, so it is not "
"computed. Declare the same full set of categories, in the same sequence, on "
"every rater variable and run again."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The grading tendency was not fitted: its model gains a parameter for every "
"pathologist, and %1$d ratings from %2$d pathologists would take minutes to "
"fit. The boundary table and the per-pathologist agreement answer the same "
"question without a model."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "The grading-tendency model could not be fitted: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The grading-tendency model did not converge (the optimizer stopped away from "
"a maximum), so no tendency is shown."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The highest observed Youden's J among the eligible candidate rules was "
"{youden}."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The highest Youden's J is {youden}, for {rule} (Agresti-Caffo 95% CI {lower} "
"to {upper}; equivalent AUC {auc}), below 0.40, the equivalent of an area "
"under the ROC curve of 0.70, under which discrimination is conventionally "
"read as poor (Hosmer, Lemeshow and Sturdivant 2013). The 95% confidence "
"interval of at least one rule reaches 0.40, so this sample cannot rule out "
"acceptable discrimination; a larger sample could settle it."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The highest Youden's J is {youden}, for {rule} (Agresti-Caffo 95% CI {lower} "
"to {upper}; equivalent AUC {auc}). No rule scored here has a 95% confidence "
"interval wholly above 0, so this sample does not show that any of them "
"separates disease-present from disease-absent cases better than chance; at "
"least one interval also reaches 0.40, the equivalent of an area under the "
"ROC curve of 0.70 (Hosmer, Lemeshow and Sturdivant 2013), so acceptable "
"discrimination is not ruled out either. The intervals are not adjusted for "
"choosing among the rules. Check the levels chosen as positive, and whether "
"the sample gives the precision you need."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"The highlight range minimum ({min}%) is not below its maximum ({max}%), so "
"no range is highlighted on the decision curve plot."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The homogeneity test (continuous ratings) asks whether the cases' {tag1}mean "
"scores{tag2} differ between clusters. Each case belongs to one cluster, so a "
"difference can come from case mix or from site-level scoring, and this "
"design cannot tell them apart. It does not test whether "
"{tag3}agreement{tag4} differs; compare the cluster-specific values and their "
"intervals for that"
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The Hosmer-Lemeshow test (Hosmer, Lemeshow & Sturdivant, 2013) used up to %s "
"groups of predicted risk (fewer where predicted risks tie; the "
"Hosmer-Lemeshow table gives the number used); a non-significant result is "
"not evidence of good calibration (Van Calster et al., 2019)."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The hull is {gap} above the empirical AUC; points between empirical "
"operating points are reachable only by randomly mixing two cut-offs (Provost "
"& Fawcett 2001); in-sample and optimistic"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The institution variance needs at least 3 institutions; this study has %d, "
"so the model was fitted without it."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The institution variance was estimated at zero: the institutions do not "
"differ detectably in case grade, so the model is in effect the one without "
"an institution term."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The institutions differ in their average rating tendency (p %s): at some "
"institutions the raters as a group rate higher or lower. This is a "
"difference in rating level, not in how well the raters agree - read the "
"cluster-specific estimates for that."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The intercept is the log-odds of the event when every selected predictor is "
"0 (or at its reference level); with the coefficients above it reproduces the "
"model's predicted probability as plogis(intercept + sum(coefficient x "
"value)). No odds ratio or importance applies to it."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The interpretation column applies this module's own cut-points to each "
"component's share of the total variance. They are a reading aid, not a "
"published standard, so judge each share against the purpose of the study."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The interval beside the apparent AUC is DeLong's 95% CI computed on these "
"fitted probabilities as if they were a fixed, pre-specified marker. It does "
"not account for fitting the coefficients or for selecting the variables and "
"lambda on the same patients, so it is centered on an optimistic estimate and "
"can under-cover the model's discrimination in new patients. Enable Bootstrap "
"Internal Validation for an optimism-corrected AUC."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The interval brackets the Weighted Bootstrap AUC in the column beside it, "
"not the AUC itself. The prior is treated as a fixed, known number, so the "
"weighting multiplies the estimate and the interval width alike by n / (n + "
"prior precision): with a prior precision of 20 and 100 complete cases the "
"interval is 83% as wide as the bootstrap interval for the AUC, and it "
"narrows further as you raise the precision. Read the AUC Summary table for "
"an interval that refers to the AUC itself."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The interval checks were run without an institution term; with one, the "
"intervals have not been checked in simulation."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The interval shown is the apparent AUC's DeLong interval; none is given for "
"the corrected AUC (Noma et al., 2021)."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The Intra-Rater and Inter-Rater tables use whichever of these two scales "
"matches the statistic named in their Statistic column, and the Analysis "
"Summary, the ICC table and the subgroup table use the same two. The scales "
"are not interchangeable: a kappa of 0.45 is Moderate, an ICC of 0.45 is "
"Poor. State which coefficient you are reporting."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The joint probabilities for %1$s are not all valid probabilities (%2$s). "
"Review the dependence parameters."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The joint probabilities for %1$s do not add back up to the test parameters "
"entered (they imply %2$.4f and %3$.4f, against %4$.4f and %5$.4f). Review "
"the dependence parameters."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The joint probabilities for %1$s sum to %2$.6f rather than 1. Review the "
"dependence parameters."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeCI.b.R
msgid ""
"The kappaSize package is required but not installed. Please install it using "
"install.packages(\"kappaSize\")"
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The kappaSize package's own search, reproduced in the Summary panel, moves "
"down in steps of 0.001 and stops once the chi-square exceeds its critical "
"value by 0.001, so it can report a value up to about 0.001 lower."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The key question is: “Are the limits of agreement clinically acceptable?” "
"For example,\n"
"                        if two Ki-67 scoring methods have LoA of ±5%, and "
"the clinical threshold is 20%,\n"
"                        a ±5% discrepancy could change treatment decisions "
"for patients near the cutoff.\n"
"                        Clinical context - not statistical significance - "
"determines acceptability."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid "The kinds of row"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The labels use the 0, 0.40, 0.60, 0.75 and 0.90 cut-points; below 0 the "
"raters disagree more than uniform random rating on the s-point scale would. "
"Finn (1970) proposed no interpretive bands for this coefficient himself. The "
"first three follow the Cicchetti (1994) reliability convention, which was "
"written for the ICC and for kappa rather than for Finn's coefficient; the "
"split at 0.90 into Excellent and Outstanding is this module's own. These are "
"not the Landis & Koch (1977) kappa bands or the Koo & Li (2016) ICC bands "
"used elsewhere in this analysis, so the same number is described differently "
"there; report the coefficient itself."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The labels use the 0.30, 0.50, 0.70 and 0.90 cut-points. Pearson (1895) "
"proposed no interpretive bands for the coefficient himself; cut-points of "
"this kind come from a widely used rule of thumb for correlation strength, of "
"the sort Mukaka (2012) sets out for medical research. Several such "
"conventions are in circulation and they disagree with one another, so report "
"the coefficient itself and say which convention you are reading it against."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The labels use the 0.30, 0.50, 0.70 and 0.90 cut-points. Spearman (1904) "
"proposed no interpretive bands for the coefficient himself; cut-points of "
"this kind come from a widely used rule of thumb for correlation strength, of "
"the sort Mukaka (2012) sets out for medical research. Several such "
"conventions are in circulation and they disagree with one another, so report "
"the coefficient itself and say which convention you are reading it against."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The labels use the 0.60, 0.75 and 0.90 cut-points. No published source "
"defines interpretive bands for specific agreement, so these are a display "
"convention of this module and not a standard; report the proportion itself."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "The latent model could not be fitted: %s."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The latent model did not converge (the optimizer stopped away from a "
"maximum), so no estimate is shown."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The latent model needs a median of at least %1$s ratings per case; this "
"study has %2$s."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The latent-class analysis has N = {n}. N < 100 is a general stability "
"warning, not a clinical adequacy threshold; precision also depends on class "
"balance, response patterns, and the number of tests. Inspect bootstrap "
"intervals and convergence, and consider collecting more observations."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The latent-model solver did not meet its numerical convergence checks after "
"%s refinement iterations. Point estimates and intervals have been left "
"blank. Try a different method, or check whether the tests nearly perfectly "
"agree or provide little information about separate classes."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The likelihood ratio for positive results was %1$s and for negative results "
"was %2$s."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The likelihood-ratio wording follows Jaeschke, Guyatt & Sackett (1994): LR+ "
"above 10 or LR- below 0.1 gives a large change in the probability of the "
"condition, 5-10 or 0.1-0.2 a moderate one, 2-5 or 0.2-0.5 a small but "
"sometimes important one, and 1-2 or 0.5-1 a small, rarely important one. The "
"bands are applied to the ratios at 3 significant figures (jamovi's default "
"number format); how much a change matters depends on the probability before "
"testing."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The limits of agreement are mean difference +/- %1$.2f x SD, the range "
"expected to contain %2$.0f%% of differences between the two methods when the "
"differences are normally distributed. That percentage is a coverage "
"proportion, not a confidence level: raising it widens the limits themselves "
"rather than expressing more uncertainty about fixed limits."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "The logistic model did not converge."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The logistic slope of %s is not estimable because the marker is constant in "
"the analyzed rows, so every patient receives the same predicted risk (the "
"sample prevalence) and there are no risk groups to compare."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The logit interval is undefined at 0% or 100% agreement, so this row shows "
"Clopper-Pearson (exact) limits."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The Low threshold (%1$.0f%%) is not below the High threshold (%2$.0f%%). The "
"lower of the two is used as Low and the higher as High; where they are equal "
"the Moderate and Low bands stay empty and cases fall into High or Poor only. "
"Set High above Low to separate the five bands."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The lower bound quoted under Interpretation adds the lower Wilson limits of "
"the rule's sensitivity and specificity and subtracts 1. It is usually more "
"conservative than the Agresti-Caffo interval shown beside Youden's J in the "
"combination table, so the two lower limits can differ."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeCI.b.R
msgid ""
"The lower limit ({lower}) drives the sample size; the expected upper limit "
"then falls at or inside {upper}."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "The marker could not be scaled to finite values."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The marker has the same value for every patient analyzed, so it cannot "
"separate the groups: its AUC is 0.5 by construction."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The marker separates the two groups perfectly in these data (AUC = 1); with "
"%1$s positive and %2$s negative cases the true AUC could still be well below "
"1."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The marker separates the two groups perfectly in these data, in the opposite "
"direction to the one set (AUC = 0); with %1$s positive and %2$s negative "
"cases the true AUC could still be well above 0."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "The maximum threshold is {max}%."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The McNemar mid-p test comparing %1$s and %2$s did not detect a significant "
"difference in diagnostic accuracy (n = %3$d paired cases; p %4$s)."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The McNemar mid-p test comparing %1$s and %2$s showed a significant "
"difference in diagnostic accuracy (n = %3$d paired cases; p %4$s)."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The McNemar mid-p test is computed directly from the binomial distribution "
"of the discordant pairs, so it has no chi-squared statistic or degrees of "
"freedom; those cells are empty in its rows."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "The mean is the plain arithmetic mean of the pairwise coefficients."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "The meta-analysis forest plot could not be built: {msg}"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The method-of-moments estimate of the rater (systematic) variance is %1$.4g. "
"A variance cannot be negative, so it was truncated to 0: these data carry no "
"evidence of a systematic offset between raters, which is not the same as "
"showing the offset is exactly zero. The systematic and random shares are "
"left empty because a share taken from a truncated component is 0%% and 100%% "
"by construction rather than by measurement. What is measured here is the "
"residual (random) variance, %2$.4g."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "the metric you chose under Optimization Metric"
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "the metric you chose under Optimization Metric (%s)"
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The minimal/poor/fair/good/excellent AUC bands are descriptive conventions, "
"not validated clinical decision thresholds. Clinical value depends on the "
"intended cutoff, uncertainty, case mix and consequences of errors."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"The minimum probability at which a patient would choose the intervention "
"over no intervention."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The minimum sensitivity and specificity settings apply only to Youden "
"optimization and were not used."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "The minimum threshold is {min}%."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The mixed model fit is singular: at least one variance component was "
"estimated at exactly zero, on the boundary of its range. That zero means the "
"data could not separate this source of variation from the others, not that "
"it is absent, so treat the estimates that rest on it with caution."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The mixed-effects comparison requires continuous numeric ratings. For "
"categorical ratings, compare conditions with the paired agreement test or "
"the subgroup analysis instead."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The model also contains a case-by-condition variance of %1$.4g (%2$.1f%% of "
"the total), which lets the ratings of one case under one condition share a "
"common deviation. The shares above are of a total that includes it, so they "
"do not add up to 100%%."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The model assumes conditional independence within each latent class. A large "
"bivariate residual is evidence against that assumption but does not "
"determine the direction of bias."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The model cannot determine which class is disease. Results are oriented so "
"that the class with the higher average selected-positive probability is the "
"high-positive class. Calling that class diseased, and the response "
"probabilities sensitivity and specificity, requires the substantive "
"assumption that selected-positive results are more common in diseased people "
"for most tests. If tests perform below chance or positive levels are "
"reversed, the clinical interpretation is inverted."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The model-based agreement needs ordered categories (at least three grades); "
"these ratings are nominal, so it is not computed. Fleiss' kappa and "
"Krippendorff's alpha remain valid for nominal ratings."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The model-based agreement places every pathologist on one common category "
"order, and the selected rater variables do not determine one, so it is not "
"computed. Declare the same full set of categories, in the same sequence, on "
"every rater variable and run again."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The model-based numbers were checked in simulation and are shown only where "
"the checks passed. A refused number is left empty and the note under the "
"table says why."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The model's apparent AUC differs from the AUC of {pred} in the AUC table "
"({auc}) because the model points the marker the way these data do (the sign "
"of its fitted slope), while the AUC table reads it in the direction set "
"under Direction."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The named scenarios are illustrative examples, not clinical guides. The "
"current row reports which scenario has the highest Youden's J and whether "
"its separately calculated Youden's J interval overlaps the current row. "
"Choosing a cut-off on the same data used to assess it is optimistically "
"biased and does not establish clinical utility."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid "The negative level \"%1$s\" is not available. Available levels: %2$s."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The negative likelihood ratio is not estimable for this table; the notices "
"above say why."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The negative-response probability and PPV are fixed at 100% by construction "
"and therefore are left blank."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid "The nomogram is omitted."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The normalized partial AUC is unavailable. The correction is undefined when "
"the partial curve falls below the diagonal; the raw partial AUC is reported "
"separately."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"The null and alternative kappa differ by only {delta}. Check that kappa0 is "
"the agreement you want to rule OUT (not the agreement you expect to observe) "
"and that kappa1 is a clinically meaningful difference from it; very close "
"values need very large studies."
msgstr ""

#: kappaSizePower/options/kappa0.description.ui
msgid ""
"The null hypothesis value of kappa - the level of agreement the study tests "
"against. kappaSize documents this as \"the null hypothesis for the kappa "
"hypothesis test\"; it is NOT the value you expect to observe."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The number needed to diagnose (1/J; Linn & Grunau 2006) was {nnd}. This is a "
"prevalence-free index of discriminating power, not a count of patients to "
"test: the number that must actually be tested to find one additional case "
"also depends on disease prevalence in the population."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The number needed to diagnose has no finite upper limit: the Youden's index "
"interval includes 0, so the interval for its inverse has no upper bound."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The number needed to diagnose is not estimable: Youden's index and its whole "
"confidence interval are at or below 0."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"The number needed to diagnose is not shown: Youden's index and its whole "
"confidence interval are at or below 0."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The number of subjects is below 25. For a binary outcome, the "
"goodness-of-fit interval this calculation assumes was found close to its "
"stated coverage from about 25 subjects when the rarer category makes up at "
"least about a fifth of the ratings (Donner and Eliasziw; Altaye, Donner and "
"Klar); for three to five categories no such figure was given, and the "
"expected cell counts are the guide (Rotondi and Donner). Below 25 subjects "
"it has not been shown to hold, so read the bound with caution."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The numerator cell of this ratio is zero, so the ratio is exactly 0 and is "
"not corrected. Its confidence interval runs from 0 to the upper limit of the "
"same log-scale interval computed with 0.5 added to every cell "
"(Haldane-Anscombe correction)."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid "The observed results involve a trade-off between sensitivity and specificity."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The one worked example that assumes conditional independence: a biochemical "
"measurement and a physical examination."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The one-df test only detects the net direction of discordances. It does not "
"distinguish the mechanisms below; use the contingency table and "
"marginal-homogeneity tests to identify them."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The one-vs-one pairwise AUC averages the AUC of every class pair, reading "
"all pairs in ONE direction: the one set under Direction, or, when Direction "
"is auto, the one that separates the outcome's first and last factor levels "
"(the extremes only if the outcome is ordered — for an unordered outcome "
"those are merely the alphabetically first and last levels, so the "
"orientation is arbitrary and Direction should be set explicitly). A pair "
"below 0.5 separates that pair in the opposite order to the one declared. "
"Because the pairs are averaged unweighted, a marker that orders the classes "
"non-monotonically averages toward 0.5 even when single pairs separate "
"strongly, so read the range of pair AUCs printed beside the mean rather than "
"the mean alone. This is not the figure pROC's multiclass.roc() prints: that "
"function orients each pair by comparing the two groups' median values on "
"these same data, which biases it upward but does not floor it at 0.5 either."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The one-vs-one pairwise AUC is defined as an unweighted average over class "
"PAIRS, so there is no prevalence-weighted version of it and none was "
"computed; the Multi-Class Average AUC table reports the unweighted pairwise "
"value instead. Choose the One-vs-Rest strategy if you want a "
"prevalence-weighted average."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The one-vs-rest Macro and Weighted AUCs are not a summary of discrimination "
"and carry no performance band. Every class is read in one shared direction, "
"so with equal class sizes the one-vs-rest AUCs always add up to half the "
"number of classes and both averages are exactly 0.5 for any marker, a "
"perfect one included; with unequal class sizes even a perfect marker can "
"average below 0.5. Judge discrimination from the one-vs-one pairwise AUCs "
"quoted in the Interpretation column (mean and range), or choose the "
"One-vs-One strategy to list every class pair."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The only rater selected is the reference rater itself. Select at least one "
"other rater to compare with the reference."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The optimal threshold maximizes the Youden index on the same data used to "
"fit the model, so sensitivity, specificity, accuracy, precision, and F1 are "
"apparent (in-sample) and optimistic. Enable bootstrap validation for an "
"optimism-corrected estimate of discrimination."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The outcome has %1$d observed levels (%2$s), but this analysis compares "
"exactly two. Filter the data to the two levels to compare, or recode the "
"outcome into two groups."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The outcome is numeric and coded 0/1, so 1 is read as the positive class. If "
"0 marks the condition, recode the outcome or set it to Nominal in Data > "
"Setup and choose 0 in Positive class."
msgstr ""

#: enhancedROC/options/validationNegative.description
msgid ""
"The outcome level that means the condition is absent. Required by the "
"frozen-model report, which refuses any outcome level that is neither this "
"nor the positive class instead of pooling it as negative."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid "The outcome variable cannot also be an explanatory variable."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The outcome variable has {n} levels, so there is no multi-class curve to "
"draw. For a two-level outcome the standard ROC results already give the "
"complete analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "The outcome variable must be binary (exactly 2 levels)."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid "The p-value is blank for: %s. The differences are still shown."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The p-values for sensitivity and specificity come from the asymptotic "
"McNemar test without continuity correction on the discordant pairs among "
"reference-positive and reference-negative cases respectively. Without "
"discordant pairs p is 1 by convention. They are not adjusted for "
"multiplicity. Overall accuracy is tested in the Statistical Comparison of "
"Test Accuracy table, so its p cell here is empty."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The p-values for sensitivity and specificity come from the McNemar mid-p "
"test on the discordant pairs among reference-positive and reference-negative "
"cases respectively. Without discordant pairs p is 1 by convention. They are "
"not adjusted for multiplicity. Overall accuracy is tested in the Statistical "
"Comparison of Test Accuracy table, so its p cell here is empty."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The paired differences, %1$s minus %2$s, were %3$s percentage points in "
"sensitivity (95%% CI %4$s to %5$s; %6$s) and %7$s percentage points in "
"specificity (95%% CI %8$s to %9$s; %10$s)."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"The pairwise p-values in this table are Holm-Bonferroni adjusted across the "
"{comparisons} pairwise comparisons that were tested (those with discordant "
"pairs). Cochran's Q is a single global test, and its p-value is not "
"adjusted. The confidence intervals and the sensitivity and specificity "
"p-values in the Differences with 95% Confidence Intervals table are not "
"adjusted for multiplicity, so an adjusted p above 0.05 can sit beside an "
"interval that excludes zero."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The partial AUC confidence interval is a bootstrap interval of the requested "
"type '%1$s' from %2$s resamples. If any resample contained a single outcome "
"class it is dropped, and a BCa interval is then reported as a percentile "
"interval instead — a notice appears when that happens. The interval is for "
"the raw partial AUC over the chosen focus range, NOT for the "
"McClish-normalized value in the next column."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid "The partial AUC is not computed; the rest of the analysis is."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The partial AUC was computed over sensitivities from %1$s to %2$s and "
"standardized with the McClish correction (McClish, 1989) to a 0.5-1 scale."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The partial AUC was computed over specificities from %1$s to %2$s and "
"standardized with the McClish correction (McClish, 1989) to a 0.5-1 scale."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The pathologist grading analysis is for categories and grades; at least one "
"rater variable is a continuous measurement, so it is not computed. Use ICC "
"or Lin's CCC for measurements."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The pathologists fall into %d groups that share no cases, so pathologist and "
"case variation cannot be separated; the model is not fitted."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The pathologists fall into %d groups that share no cases, so their grading "
"tendencies cannot be compared on one scale; the model is not fitted."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid "The patient ID must be a separate identifier, not an outcome or predictor."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The pattern-type and statistic filters affect the plots only. The "
"performance tables always show every row."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The pattern-type filter \"{filter}\" matches none of the rows in this "
"analysis, so the bar chart, heatmap and forest plot are blank. Pattern "
"filters apply to exact result patterns such as \"+/+\"; a single-test "
"analysis has no such rows, and the single-test and strategy rows are not "
"result patterns. Set the pattern filter back to \"All Patterns\" to see the "
"plots."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid "The penalized model retained no informative terms at the chosen lambda. %s"
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The penalized-EM analysis has N = {n}. N < 50 is a general stability "
"warning, not a clinical adequacy threshold; fixed priors may have "
"substantial influence. Inspect bootstrap intervals and consider collecting "
"more observations."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The plain-language summary describes Cohen's or Fleiss' kappa, which is not "
"computed for continuous measurements. For measurements, read the ICC, Lin's "
"CCC and the Bland-Altman limits of agreement, if selected, which carry their "
"own interpretation notes."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The positive class (cases) was %1$s and the negative class (non-cases) was "
"%2$s."
msgstr ""

