#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"The published formula behind this sample size is a large-sample "
"approximation that treats the expected proportions as known. In {R} "
"simulated studies of {n} subjects analyzed with the recommended "
"goodness-of-fit test (prevalence estimated by maximum likelihood with kappa "
"held at kappa0), the power to reject kappa0 when kappa equals kappa1 was "
"about {sim}, short of the stated {power}."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"The published formula behind this sample size treats the expected "
"proportions as known and rests on a large-sample approximation. With three "
"or more raters and three to five categories, simulations of the recommended "
"goodness-of-fit test at the published sample size gave a power of about 0.64 "
"to 0.81 for a stated 0.80, below 0.75 in 8 of 12 designs, balanced "
"proportions included, so this design can fall well short of the stated power "
"of {power} at {n} subjects. How far short cannot be computed reliably here, "
"so confirm the sample size by simulating the intended analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid "The ranking"
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The rarest category is expected in {share} of ratings, so each rater is "
"expected to place only about {k} of the {n} subjects in it, fewer than one. "
"The goodness-of-fit approximation behind the expected lower bound of {bound} "
"does not hold with so few, and with a category this rare the model allows "
"little or no agreement below zero, so the bound stays near or above zero "
"however few the subjects are: it cannot support a dependable conclusion. "
"Enrol more subjects, or enrich the case series so that the rare category is "
"more common."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeCI.b.R
#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"The rarest category is expected in {share} of ratings, so each rater is "
"expected to place only about {k} of the {n} subjects in it. Kappa depends "
"strongly on prevalence (Byrt, Bishop and Carlin): when a category is this "
"rare, high raw agreement can coexist with a low kappa, the estimate rests on "
"those few subjects, and a kappa measured here cannot be assumed to "
"generalize to a setting where the category is more common. Report the "
"prevalence and the category-specific agreement (the proportion of that "
"category's ratings that another rater matched) beside kappa."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The raters gave identical ratings to every case, so the residual variance is "
"zero: the F test, its p value and the confidence interval are undefined and "
"have been left blank."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "The raters never disagreed, so there is no direction to test."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The raters' ratings differ only by a constant offset per rater, so the "
"residual variance is zero: the F test, its p value and the confidence "
"interval are undefined and have been left blank."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The ratings run from %1$d to %2$d, but the number of rating categories is "
"set to %3$d. Finn's coefficient divides by the variance of a uniform rating "
"on an s-point scale, so this setting moves the value in both directions: set "
"larger than the scale the ratings actually use, it pulls the coefficient "
"towards 1 and can turn poor agreement into outstanding agreement; set "
"smaller, it deflates the coefficient and can drive it negative. Set it to "
"the number of categories the rating scale really has, or declare the rater "
"variables as ordinal factors carrying all their levels, in which case the "
"scale is read from the data and this setting is ignored."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
