msgid ""
"The results describe agreement with the majority-vote rule and are not "
"estimates of diagnostic accuracy."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "The ROC analysis used the following specifications."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The ROC tables read %1$s in the direction in which it ranks patients well "
"(AUC %3$.3f), but its group means point the other way, so the slope of the "
"fitted logistic risk of %2$s runs opposite to that direction. This happens "
"with a skewed marker or an outlying value. Calibration, the Brier score, the "
"Hosmer-Lemeshow test, the decision and clinical impact curves and internal "
"validation use that fitted model, the risk model the data support, which "
"ranks patients the opposite way to the ROC, cut-point and fixed-cutoff "
"tables; they do not describe %1$s as those tables read it, and can disagree "
"with them. A log or rank transformation of the marker usually removes the "
"conflict."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The rows are not independent: every rule is scored on the same patients, so "
"the differences between them are correlated. A formal comparison needs a "
"paired procedure -- McNemar's test applied SEPARATELY within the diseased "
"patients to compare sensitivity and within the non-diseased patients to "
"compare specificity, because a single McNemar over the whole sample compares "
"positivity rates rather than accuracy. Paired PPV and NPV comparisons need a "
"generalised score test instead. The optional table \"Combination Rules vs "
"Each Test Alone\" (option \"Combinations vs each test alone\") makes this "
"comparison within each reference group for every combination strategy "
"(Parallel, Serial and Majority) against each test it combines: a Parallel or "
"Serial strategy can move only one way, so the table gives its change with an "
"interval, and McNemar's test is used for Majority. Paired PPV and NPV "
"comparisons are not performed."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"The rule that separates the two groups best in this sample is one that calls "
"a patient positive when the tests are negative; or every named rule (each "
"test alone and every strategy) performs at or below chance while an exact "
"result pattern does not. Both are the signature of a reversed positive "
"level: the arithmetic still works, but the sensitivity and specificity "
"reported for each rule then describe results read the wrong way round, and "
"the best-performing rule is one no clinician would apply. Check the level "
"chosen as positive for the reference standard and for each test before "
"interpreting any of these results."
msgstr ""

