#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The Shapiro-Wilk test of the differences was not run: shapiro.test() accepts "
"at most 5000 observations. Normality of the differences is therefore "
"UNTESTED, and the Limits of Agreement below assume it. Inspect the "
"Bland-Altman plot instead."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"The significance level ({alpha}) must be below the power ({power}). A study "
"whose power does not exceed its type I error rate provides no evidence, and "
"the sample size cannot be computed for it. Conventional values are alpha "
"0.05 and power 0.80."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The similarity shown is Lin's concordance correlation coefficient between "
"the two raters: 1.00 means their readings are interchangeable, 0.00 means no "
"concordance at all, and a negative value means they move in opposite "
"directions. It falls when two raters scatter around each other and also when "
"one reads consistently higher or on a wider scale, so unlike a correlation "
"it is comparable across datasets."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The smaller outcome group has %1$s patients (%2$s positive, %3$s negative); "
"affected: %4$s. DeLong's interval is a large-sample approximation: for a "
"single AUC it covers the true value less often than its stated level when "
"the smaller group is small, and more so the closer the AUC is to 1 or 0 "
"(Obuchowski & Lieber 1998: more than 200 patients for high-accuracy areas). "
"Feng, Cortese & Baumgartner (2017) found that interval methods for the AUC "
"disagree most when the AUC is high and the sample small. In this module's "
"validation simulation, which drew equal-variance binormal data, a 95%% "
"DeLong interval covered a true AUC of 0.80 in 91%% of datasets with 15 "
"positive and 15 negative cases, and a true AUC of 0.95 in 87%% with 30 and "
"30 and in 93%% with 100 and 100; these are averages over all datasets of "
"each size, and with unequal variances or skewed data they were about 90%%, "
"84%% and 92%%. Coverage followed the smaller group's size times the distance "
"of the AUC from 1 (from 0 for an AUC below 0.5): it was about 93%% wherever "
"that product was 6, so this warning appears when the smaller group has fewer "
"than 30 patients or that product is below 6 (fewer than 60 patients at an "
"AUC of 0.90, fewer than 120 at 0.95); the cut-off is a heuristic. • Read the "
"interval as approximate and probably too narrow. • Bootstrap confidence "
"intervals are an alternative, but no method is exact at this size, and no "
"bootstrap interval can be formed when the groups are perfectly separated."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"The smaller outcome group has %1$s patients, so cross-validation used %2$s "
"folds instead of 10: every fold must hold patients from both groups for its "
"AUC to exist. With so few patients per fold the fold AUCs vary widely."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
