"The tests were evaluated on different sets of cases, so this ranking "
"compares them on the {n} cases with a determinate result for every test; the "
"per-test figures use each test's own available cases."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"The threshold range you entered, {lo}% to {hi}%, is narrower than the step "
"size of {step}%, so it produces only one threshold ({one}%) and no decision "
"curve can be drawn."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The time-point separator is empty, so the rater and the time point cannot be "
"told apart in the column names. Enter the character that separates them, for "
"example an underscore for columns named Rater1_Time1 and Rater1_Time2."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The two likelihood ratios in the table cannot be drawn together on one "
"nomogram."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The two rater variables declare different category sets. q is taken from the "
"larger of the two, not from both combined - combining them would count the "
"same clinical category twice and inflate PABAK. Give both variables the same "
"levels if they are meant to share a scale."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The two raters placed every case in the same category, so there are no "
"discordant cases: their category totals are identical and the test statistic "
"is undefined (0 divided by 0)."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The two raters use rating categories with significantly different "
"frequencies (%s). This indicates systematic bias - one rater may be more "
"lenient or strict than the other. Examine the marginal frequency tables to "
"identify which categories show the largest discrepancies. Consider this "
"alongside Cohen's kappa to distinguish bias from poor agreement."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The two raters use rating categories with significantly different "
"frequencies (%s). This indicates systematic change or bias in category usage "
"between the two conditions/raters. Examine marginal frequency tables to "
"identify which categories show the largest shifts. Consider this alongside "
"Cohen's kappa to distinguish systematic bias from poor agreement."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"The unpenalized companion fit did not converge, so the bivariate residuals "
"could not be computed. Penalized EM assumes the tests err independently "
"within each class; run the latent class analysis and check its residuals "
"before interpreting these estimates."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The unpenalized refit on all candidate variables could not be fitted (%s), "
"so its row is omitted."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The unpenalized refit on the retained terms could not be fitted (%s), so its "
"row is omitted."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeCI.b.R
msgid ""
"The upper limit ({upper}) drives the sample size; the expected lower limit "
"then falls at or inside {lower}."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"The upper limit of the number needed to diagnose is not shown: the Youden's "
"index interval includes 0, so the interval for its inverse has no upper "
"bound."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid "the upper quartile"
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid "the upper tertile"
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"The user-supplied population prevalence is used, not the prevalence observed "
"in this sample."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The validated kappa sample size formulas cover 2 to 6 raters, but {raters} "
"were requested. Enter 6 or fewer raters, or size the study by another "
"method; the ICC option accepts more raters but needs ratings that can be "
"treated as numeric."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The variance decomposition places every rater on one common category order, "
"and the selected variables do not determine one. Either they contradict each "
"other, or - more often - they simply never place some pair of categories "
"relative to each other: if one variable declares Absent and Diffuse and "
"another declares Absent and Focal, nothing in either declaration says "
"whether Focal comes before or after Diffuse. Merging them would invent an "
"order, and the rater offset this table reports would then depend on which "
"variable was selected first. Set every rater variable to the same ordered "
"scale, declaring the same full set of categories in the same sequence, and "
"run again."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The variance decomposition splits disagreement into a rater offset and "
"residual noise, which needs one common numeric scale for every rater. Use "
"rater columns that are all continuous, or all ordered factors declaring the "
"same categories in the same order. An unordered (nominal) variable has no "
"scale for an offset to sit on: set its measure type to Ordinal if its "
"categories really do run from low to high, otherwise use Fleiss' kappa or "
"Krippendorff's alpha. Continuous and ordered-factor columns cannot be mixed "
"here because the two scales are not in the same units."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"The word beside strict accuracy uses the 0.70, 0.80 and 0.90 cut-points. No "
"published source defines interpretive bands for an accuracy, so these are a "
"display convention of this module and not a standard; report the accuracy "
"itself."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"The worked example replaced values you supplied: %s. A worked example sets "
"every parameter; to use your own numbers set the worked example to Custom "
"values."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"the Youden index (sensitivity + specificity - 1) computed under a "
"normal-distribution model for the two groups; the Optimization Metric "
"setting is not used by this method"
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"the Youden index (sensitivity + specificity - 1) read off a kernel-smoothed "
"ROC curve; the Optimization Metric setting is not used by this method"
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"The Youden search did find a cut point (%1$s and above, sensitivity %2$s, "
"specificity %3$s), but the score points the wrong way, so calling the high "
"scores high risk inverts the real risk."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"There is no true positive at this threshold, so patients screened per true "
"positive is undefined."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid "These are bounds for how ties are resolved, not a confidence interval."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"These are fixed-prior penalized-EM point estimates, not posterior summaries. "
"Accuracy terminology also assumes that the high-positive latent class "
"represents disease."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"These are single-cohort estimates. Their practical meaning depends on "
"intended use, error consequences, prevalence, patient spectrum, and "
"reference-standard quality. This educational summary is not a clinical guide."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"These are Wilson score 95% confidence intervals. The decision and "
"decisioncompare analyses report exact (Clopper-Pearson) intervals for the "
"same proportions, which are usually a little wider, so the two sets of "
"limits differ."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"These bands are a rough teaching scale, not a clinical standard; other "
"authors label the same ranges differently (Hosmer, Lemeshow & Sturdivant "
"2013 call 0.7-0.8 acceptable, 0.8-0.9 excellent and 0.9 or more outstanding)."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"These cases carry a level that codes missingness rather than a result. They "
"are excluded rather than counted as negative, which would have biased "
"sensitivity and specificity."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"These cases carry a missingness code. They are excluded from agreement "
"denominators rather than counted as negative."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"These cut-points are this module's display convention for describing the "
"share in words - they have no published source and no diagnostic status, so "
"read the share and the two variance components rather than the row you land "
"in."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid "These dependence values are the analysis defaults, not estimates from data."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"These entries under Custom cutoffs are not numbers and were ignored: %s. "
"Separate cutoffs with commas and use a point as the decimal mark (0,5 is "
"read as the two cutoffs 0 and 5); leave out units and percent signs."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"These estimates are apparent performance in this dataset and have not been "
"externally validated."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"These figures are APPARENT (in-sample) and optimistic twice over: the points "
"were derived from a model fitted to this dataset, and the score cutoff was "
"chosen to maximize the Youden index on the same rows. They are not an "
"estimate of how the score would perform on new patients. Enable bootstrap "
"validation for an optimism-corrected estimate of the model, and validate any "
"score externally before clinical use."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"These intervals may be conservative: in simulation with at least 100 cases "
"and 3 pathologists, the 95% interval covered the true value in 92 to 99.5% "
"of studies (mean 95.4%), so it is never much too narrow and sometimes wider "
"than necessary."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"These intervals treat each count as one independent patient. If a patient "
"contributes several slides, blocks or lesions, they can be too narrow: count "
"one result per patient, or use a method for clustered data."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "These labels describe discrimination in this sample only."
msgstr ""

#: R/cotest.b.R
msgid ""
"These likelihood ratios describe each test <i>on its own</i>. Because the "
"two tests are modeled as conditionally dependent, the combined likelihood "
"ratio is <b>not</b> their product — use the Post-test Odds column of the "
"Co-Testing Results table instead."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "These look like percentages: enter {values} instead."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"These manual cut point entries were not used: %s. Each must be "
"'variable=value' using an explanatory variable's name as written; a name "
"containing a comma, semicolon or '=' cannot be given a manual cut."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"These markers take the same value in every case and carry no discrimination "
"information: %s. Their AUC of 0.500 has zero empirical variance; the AUC "
"summary withholds the interval and p-value. No clinically useful cutpoint "
"can distinguish the classes. Remove them from the analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"These pairwise results are exploratory because the global Cochran's Q test "
"was not significant."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"These quantities describe agreement with the selected comparator. The tables "
"state the denominators and interval methods. Differences between tests "
"require paired comparisons on common cases."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"These rater columns are numeric but every value is a whole number no greater "
"than 10, which is what a rating scale looks like rather than a measurement. "
"The decomposition has treated them as an equal-interval scale, so one step "
"between adjacent codes counts the same everywhere. That is reasonable for an "
"ordinal grade recorded as 1, 2, 3 and meaningless for nominal categories "
"that merely happen to be numbered, and nothing in the data distinguishes the "
"two. If these codes are nominal, read Fleiss' kappa or Krippendorff's alpha "
"instead of this table."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"These ratings are categorical, so the distance used is 1 minus the "
"proportion of cases on which two raters chose the same category. The "
"Concordance, Correlation, Euclidean and Manhattan settings apply to "
"continuous measurements and leave this table unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"These ratings are categorical, so the distance used is 1 minus the "
"proportion of raters who gave two cases the same category. The Correlation, "
"Euclidean and Manhattan settings apply to continuous measurements and leave "
"this table unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"These rows have a 2-by-2 cell count below 5 (smallest cell {minimum}): "
"{rows}. Their likelihood ratios, diagnostic odds ratios and confidence "
"intervals rest on very few cases and may be unstable even when they look "
"informative. At such counts the log-scale intervals for these ratios are "
"approximate: their actual coverage can be above or below the nominal 95%, "
"and where the 0.5 continuity correction was applied it can fall below it. "
"Sensitivity, specificity and Youden's J on the same rows can still be sound, "
"because those depend on the size of the two reference groups rather than on "
"the smallest cell. Treat the ratio columns as exploratory and validate them "
"in a larger independent sample."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"These rules have no estimate with a confidence interval for the statistic "
"named, so their row is left empty in that facet: {details}. This happens for "
"a pattern that no patient shows (PPV, LR+ and the diagnostic odds ratio are "
"undefined there, and LR- is exactly 1 with no interval) and for a rule on "
"which every patient, or no patient, tests positive. The tables show these "
"cells, with the reason in their notes."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"These tables show the {tag1}raw frequency distribution{tag2} of ratings "
"across raters.\n"
"                        For 2 raters, a cross-tabulation (contingency table) "
"is displayed. For 3+ raters,\n"
"                        all unique rating combinations and their frequencies "
"are shown."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"These values describe agreement with a majority-vote rule built from the "
"same tests. They are not estimates of diagnostic accuracy."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"They are computed correctly at those exact values, but they are not shown on "
"the decision curve, and the surrounding rows come from the analysed range."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"They are imprecise below about 200 patients with and 200 without the event "
"(Van Calster et al. 2019); this sample has %1$s with and %2$s without. "
"Sampling noise alone inflates them, so a well-calibrated model can look "
"miscalibrated, and a small value is no proof of good calibration either."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"They are Wald intervals on the AUC scale: the AUC plus or minus z times "
"DeLong's standard error, truncated to 0-1."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"They cover each threshold separately and are not a simultaneous band; choose "
"the simultaneous band to read the whole curve at once."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"They describe that probability; they are not a judgement of whether a marker "
"is fit for patient care."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid "They were all computed at the prevalence of this sample."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"They would like to know how low the one-sided {conf}% lower confidence bound "
"for kappa can be expected to fall - the smallest agreement the study would "
"still be unable to rule out; every value below it is excluded."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"This analysis cannot tell whether the predicted risks, or a clinical rule, "
"were developed on these same rows, and it applies no optimism correction."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"This analysis compares two or three tests against the same reference. Select "
"a second test variable in another test slot; for a single test use the "
"Medical Decision analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This analysis estimates latent-class parameters or describes agreement with "
"a reference rule built from the selected tests. Interpretation depends on "
"the chosen method and its assumptions."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This analysis evaluated %1$d predictor(s) using %2$d observations in a %3$s "
"context."
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"This analysis evaluates diagnostic test performance by comparing test "
"results to a gold standard (reference). It calculates key diagnostic "
"accuracy measures including sensitivity, specificity, predictive values, and "
"likelihood ratios."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"This analysis reports an {tag1}empirical Total Deviation Index (TDI){tag2}: "
"the selected"
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This analysis resamples the data by the bootstrap and shrinks the result "
"towards the prior AUC. It does not run MCMC and it does not produce a "
"Bayesian posterior: the interval shown is a bootstrap percentile interval, "
"not a credible interval. The Bootstrap Evidence Ratio is a resampling "
"frequency - the number of bootstrap resamples whose AUC exceeded 0.5, "
"divided by one plus the number that did not - and is not a Bayes factor, "
"because no marginal likelihood is computed. Do not read it against Bayes "
"factor evidence thresholds. For genuine Bayesian inference use dedicated "
"software such as Stan or JAGS."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"This analysis scores two or three diagnostic tests against the reference "
"standard on the same patients: every exact result pattern, each test alone, "
"and the usual ways of combining them -- Parallel and Serial across all the "
"tests and, with three tests, across every pair, plus Majority. Mixed rules "
"such as \"Test 1 positive and either Test 2 or Test 3 positive\" are not "
"listed; the ranking says when some other way of calling the result patterns "
"positive would score higher. With a single test selected it reports that one "
"test alone (a single-test row, described below); the pattern and strategy "
"rows do not arise."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"This analysis simultaneously assesses two critical aspects of measurement "
"reliability:"
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This analysis uses the %1$d of %2$d cases (%3$.1f%%) with a result recorded "
"for every selected test. All displayed estimates use these complete cases. "
"If the probability of a missing result is related to an unobserved test "
"result or latent class, the estimates can be biased."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This AUC is apparent (in-sample), and where a cutpoint was searched for on "
"these data the cutpoint metrics are optimistic. Independent validation is "
"needed before these figures describe performance in new patients."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "This binary analysis does not account for censoring."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"This bound is for the model-based goodness-of-fit interval; the Wald, "
"Fisher-z or bootstrap intervals that agreement software usually reports for "
"Cohen's or Fleiss' kappa can give a different lower limit for the same data. "
"Report the goodness-of-fit interval, or plan for the interval that will be "
"reported."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"This calculation assumes all %1$d categories are <b>equally common</b> (each "
"%2$.0f%% of cases). Pathology grading distributions are rarely balanced, and "
"the required sample size rises steeply as they skew: for a 3-category scale "
"at kappa 0.40 vs 0.60 with 2 raters, equal thirds need about 106 cases, a "
"70/20/10 split about 155, and an 85/10/5 split about 264. Treat the number "
"above as a <b>lower bound</b> and inflate it for the expected prevalence of "
"your categories."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeCI.b.R
msgid ""
"This combination of expected proportions and rater count is too extreme for "
"the kappaSize engine: with {raters} raters and a rarest category of "
"{rarest}, some agreement patterns are so unlikely that the calculation loses "
"them to rounding and never converges. About {n} subjects would be needed. "
"Use fewer raters, or a less extreme expected prevalence — the same design "
"usually computes with one rater fewer."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"This computational safeguard does not imply that 10 observations are "
"statistically adequate."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid "This design cannot support a reliability claim."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"This does not establish that the tests perform equally; no test of one test "
"against another across a pre-specified equivalence margin was performed. (If "
"requested, the OPA criterion column compares each test with a user-specified "
"descriptive threshold; it is not a noninferiority comparison between tests "
"or clinical guidance.)"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid "This estimate is not available for this design."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"This exploratory test regresses the paired difference on the pair mean. "
"Because the mean is built from the same two measurements as the difference, "
"a non-zero slope also appears whenever the methods differ in precision, even "
"without magnitude-dependent bias. A significant result therefore means "
"'difference-mean association or unequal precision', not proven proportional "
"bias. Confirm it with replicated measurements or a justified "
"errors-in-variables model before making a proportional-bias claim."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncalculator.b.R
msgid ""
"This function evaluates a diagnostic test against a stated reference "
"standard. It calculates sensitivity, specificity, predictive values, and "
"likelihood ratios. Reference standards can be imperfect, so disagreement may "
"reflect error in either method."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "This indicates a data processing error; please report it."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeCI.b.R
msgid ""
"This is a CONFIDENCE-INTERVAL calculation: it returns the number of subjects\n"
"needed for the expected confidence limits around kappa to reach the "
"requested\n"
"limits. It answers a different question from the power approach\n"
"(kappaSizePower), which sizes a study to reject a null value, so the two\n"
"generally give different sample sizes for the same study - choose the one "
"that\n"
"matches how the result will be reported."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"This is a descriptive ranking of {n} candidate rule(s) scored on at least 10 "
"disease-present and 10 disease-absent cases with a Youden's J above zero; no "
"significance test or multiplicity correction was applied."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"This is a descriptive, sample-dependent ranking of exact-pattern rules, each "
"test alone and named testing strategies. It is not a clinical guide or "
"validated recommendation."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeCI.b.R
#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid "This is a planning expectation, not a guarantee."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"This is a POWER calculation: it returns the number of subjects needed to "
"REJECT kappa0 in favor of kappa1 in a two-sided test at the stated "
"significance level and power."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This is an in-sample estimate with no external validation behind it, and it "
"is additionally optimistic because this predictor was picked as the "
"highest-scoring of %d; the Internal Validation section reports its internal "
"validation."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This is an in-sample estimate with no external validation behind it; the "
"Internal Validation section reports its internal validation."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This is an in-sample estimate with no internal or external validation behind "
"it, and it is additionally optimistic because this predictor was picked as "
"the highest-scoring of %d."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This is an in-sample estimate with no internal or external validation behind "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"This is an unconditional empirical quantile of the observed absolute "
"differences with a distribution-free (order-statistic) confidence interval. "
"It does not model heteroscedasticity, adjust for case mix, predict a "
"guaranteed proportion of future measurements, or by itself establish method "
"equivalence or regulatory acceptability."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "This is not a confirmatory test."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This is not an accuracy estimate: each test helps build the rule against "
"which it is evaluated, creating incorporation bias. Strict majority means "
"more than half positive; with an even number of tests, tied cases are rule "
"negative. With two tests this is identical to the all-tests-positive rule."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncompare.b.R
msgid ""
"This is not evidence that the two tests are equivalent: McNemar's test uses "
"only the discordant pairs, so with few discordances it has little power to "
"detect a real difference. Check the confidence interval for the accuracy "
"difference to see how large a difference remains compatible with these "
"data.</p>"
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This is not posterior sampling, priors are not configurable, intervals are "
"not credible intervals, and latent-class labels remain unidentified."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"This is the bound the study reaches if the agreement it observes lands "
"exactly on kappa0 and its category proportions match the anticipated ones. "
"Roughly half of such studies will observe less agreement than anticipated "
"and end with a lower bound below the figure shown, so read it as a planning "
"expectation rather than a guarantee."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This is the fitted proportion of the high-positive latent class. It is not "
"automatically disease prevalence because latent-class labels are "
"unidentified."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This is the proportion of analyzed cases meeting the selected reference "
"rule, not an estimate of disease prevalence."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid "This is what you specified (Direction = \"%s\")."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid "this marker"
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid "This method cannot estimate accuracy"
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This method defines a case as rule positive only when every selected test is "
"positive."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This method defines a case as rule positive when at least one selected test "
"is positive."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This method defines rule-positive cases by majority vote of the selected "
"tests."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This method fits two unobserved classes and estimates each test's "
"selected-positive probability within each class."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"This method maximizes the two-class conditional-independence log likelihood "
"plus the sum of log sensitivities and log specificities. These fixed "
"Beta(2,1) penalties favor higher accuracy; a uniform Beta(1,1) penalty is "
"used for the class proportion. Multiple EM initializations and numerical "
"refinement produce a MAP point estimate, not a posterior sample."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"this method scores each test against a reference built from the tests "
"themselves, so the figures describe agreement with that rule rather than "
"diagnostic accuracy."
msgstr ""

#: R/psychopdaROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This metric counts every false positive and every false negative at an equal "
"unit cost (and every correct classification at an equal unit utility), so it "
"selects the same cut-point as accuracy. Cost ratio (FP:FN) is not used here; "
"to weigh the two errors unequally, choose the Cost-benefit optimized "
"cutpoint method."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"This model assigns every case the same predicted probability, so it does not "
"discriminate at all and no meaningful threshold exists. A default of 0.500 "
"was used, which classifies every case into one group: the resulting "
"sensitivity of 1.000 (or specificity of 1.000, depending on which side of "
"0.500 that constant probability falls) and the F1 score are artefacts of "
"that, not evidence of a sensitive or specific test. Only the AUC of 0.500 is "
"informative here."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"This numeric outcome is not coded 0/1, so neither value can be assumed to be "
"the event. Set the outcome's measure type to Nominal, then choose the 'Event "
"level'."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"This only ranks the models against each other; it does not mean the model is "
"useful."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This outcome is numeric with %s distinct values, so it looks like a "
"measurement rather than a two-level outcome (condition present / absent). "
"Choose a different outcome variable, or a 0/1 variable made from this one at "
"a clinically justified cut-off."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This outcome is numeric with values %s; only a 0/1 outcome is read without a "
"choice (1 = positive). Set its measure type to Nominal in Data > Setup so "
"Positive class lists its values, or recode it 0/1."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"This plot was saved by an earlier analysis version. Re-run the analysis to "
"create updated plotting coordinates, then save the document again."
msgstr ""

#: R/lassologistic.b.R
msgid ""
"This point scale gives a single factor up to %d points, more than the 20 the "
"Max-scaled method allows, which makes the total hard to add up at the "
"bedside. Smallest-unit widens the scale when one selected predictor has a "
"far smaller effect than the others; Beta10 does when a predictor's effect is "
"large. Max-scaled caps the strongest predictor at the 'Maximum points per "
"feature' you set."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid "This range contains a gap where the model is not superior; read the curve."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"This represents: Of all cases either rater called positive, what proportion "
"did both raters agree were positive?"
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"This row pools every rater × time-point column as though it were a separate "
"rater: N Raters counts %1$d reads made by %2$d raters, not %1$d raters. "
"Repeated reads by the same rater are correlated, which violates the "
"independence assumption behind Fleiss' kappa and ICC(2,1) and generally "
"makes inter-rater agreement look better than it is. Treat this as a "
"descriptive summary over all reads; for a defensible inter-rater "
"coefficient, restrict the analysis to one time point per rater."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"This sample has {pos} disease-present and {neg} disease-absent complete "
"cases. Sensitivity and PPV are limited by the disease-present count and "
"specificity and NPV by the disease-absent count, so with fewer than 10 in "
"one group the statistics that depend on it are driven by single patients and "
"their confidence intervals span most of the possible range. Every likelihood "
"ratio and diagnostic odds ratio inherits that instability."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid "This sample size cannot demonstrate agreement."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"This sample size is for <b>unweighted</b> kappa: categories are treated as "
"nominal and every disagreement counts the same. It does not apply to "
"weighted kappa for ordered grades, so if the Weighted kappa option is set to "
"linear or squared weights, either analyze the study unweighted as well or "
"size it with a dedicated weighted-kappa method."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeCI.b.R
msgid ""
"This sample size is planned for the model-based goodness-of-fit interval; it "
"does not establish the requested precision for the Wald, Fisher-z or "
"bootstrap intervals that agreement software usually reports for Cohen's or "
"Fleiss' κ. Report the goodness-of-fit interval, or size the study for the "
"interval that will be reported."
msgstr ""

