"With %1$s patients in the smaller group DeLong's interval is approximate and "
"tends to be too narrow, so a marker carrying no information at all is "
"flagged here somewhat more often than the confidence level implies."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncurve.b.R
msgid ""
"With a step of {step}%, the threshold grid starting at {lo}% stops at "
"{last}%, so thresholds above it, up to the requested maximum of {hi}%, are "
"not analysed."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"With an anticipated kappa of {kappa0} and these proportions, even 1,000,000 "
"subjects, up to six raters and a significance level of 0.20 (the largest "
"this analysis allows) leave the expected lower bound below 0.40, so changing "
"the sample size, the raters or the significance level cannot support a claim "
"of moderate or better reliability here."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"with Direction set to auto a marker carrying no information lands here about "
"1 in 9 times at 10 positive and 10 negative patients, rising toward 1 in 6 "
"in large samples; only a confidence interval wholly below 0.50 shows that "
"the marker runs the other way (check Positive class, then Direction)"
msgstr ""

#: R/decision.b.R
msgid ""
"With fewer than 20 cases each proportion rests on a handful of patients, so "
"one reclassified case moves sensitivity or specificity by several percentage "
"points and the 95% confidence intervals (tick 95% confidence intervals) will "
"be very wide. Read the intervals rather than the point estimates. How many "
"cases are enough depends on the precision you need at the expected "
"prevalence (Buderer 1996), not on a fixed total."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"With fewer than 20 complete cases every proportion rests on a handful of "
"patients, so one reclassified case moves sensitivity or specificity by "
"several percentage points and the 95% confidence intervals are very wide. "
"Read the intervals rather than the point estimates. The number of cases a "
"diagnostic accuracy study needs depends on the expected sensitivity and "
"specificity, the precision wanted and the prevalence (Buderer 1996); as a "
"rough rule of thumb the intervals seldom narrow usefully below about 100 "
"cases, and combination analysis splits those cases across four or eight "
"patterns."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"With k raters you compute C(k,2) p-values. For k=4 that is 6 tests; for\n"
"                        k=6 it is 15. Enable a multiple-testing correction "
"(Bonferroni, BH, or\n"
"                        Holm) under {tag1}Mixed-Effects Comparison → "
"Multiple Testing\n"
"                        Correction{tag2} to control familywise error or FDR."
msgstr ""

#: R/decisioncombine.b.R
msgid ""
"With one test there is only one rule to score, the test itself, so no "
"ranking table is shown; the combination table reports that test on its own. "
"Select a second test to rank the combinations."
msgstr ""

#: R/enhancedROC.b.R
msgid ""
"With only %1$s positive and %2$s negative patients (fewer than 10 in a "
"class), the estimates below are unreliable."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"With only a few clusters, any between-cluster variance and any test of it "
"rest on very little information"
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"With the sample size already fixed, this reports the expected lower bound of "
"the one-sided {level} goodness-of-fit confidence interval for the intraclass "
"(Fleiss-type) kappa of the common-correlation model, on the models of Donner "
"and Eliasziw (two raters, binary outcome), Altaye, Donner and Klar (binary "
"outcome, more raters) and Altaye, Donner and Eliasziw (three to five "
"categories), in the confidence-interval approach of Rotondi and Donner "
"implemented in the kappaSize package; for two raters with equal marginal "
"frequencies this kappa coincides with Cohen's kappa."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"With three or more raters and three to five categories, this sample size can "
"fall well short of power {power}; confirm it by simulating the intended "
"analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"With three or more raters each interval is kappa plus or minus the t "
"quantile on n - 1 degrees of freedom times Gwet's subject-sampling standard "
"error (irrCAC), where n is the number of cases with at least one rating (the "
"N Cases column). The raters are treated as fixed, so the interval reflects "
"the sampling of cases, not of raters. The kappa interval method option (Wald "
"or Fisher z) applies to Cohen's kappa for two raters only and does not "
"change these intervals."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"With three or more raters, do not compute the chi-square over every count of "
"raters calling the finding present (none, one, ..., all): that statistic has "
"more than one degree of freedom and, read on one, rejects a true kappa0 much "
"more often than the stated significance level."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"With three or more raters, estimate the category prevalences by maximum "
"likelihood with kappa held at kappa0, as Altaye, Donner and Eliasziw do; "
"plugging in the overall proportions of ratings makes the test reject a true "
"kappa0 too often."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeFixedN.b.R
msgid ""
"With three or more raters, the lower bound for a binary finding also depends "
"on how agreement among more than two raters is modeled. This analysis uses "
"the model of Altaye, Donner and Klar; the Dirichlet-multinomial model used "
"for three to five categories, which for two categories is the beta-binomial, "
"describes the same kappa, and with the proportions held at their anticipated "
"values it never gave a lower bound than the one shown in the designs checked "
"and usually gave a higher one (by up to about 0.10 when the finding is rare "
"and the study small), so the figure shown is the more cautious of the two."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizeCI.b.R
msgid ""
"With three or more raters, the sample size for a binary finding also depends "
"on how agreement among more than two raters is modeled. This analysis uses "
"the model of Altaye, Donner and Klar; the Dirichlet-multinomial model used "
"for three to five categories, which for two categories is the beta-binomial, "
"describes the same κ but usually needs fewer subjects (in the designs "
"checked, as few as about half when the finding is rare), so the figure shown "
"is generally the more cautious of the two."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"With three tests the nominal residual degrees of freedom are zero, but the "
"latent-class model need not reproduce the observed table. These residuals "
"describe discrepancies; they are not chi-squared tests of conditional "
"independence. A small residual does not establish the assumption."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"With three tests the two-class model has as many parameters as the data have "
"free cell frequencies, so it usually reproduces the observed table exactly "
"and conditional independence cannot be checked from these data. If two of "
"the tests share a mechanism (same target, same reader or same specimen), the "
"estimates can be strongly biased in either direction with no sign of it in "
"the fit statistics or the bivariate residuals. Detecting such dependence "
"needs at least four tests."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"With three tests there are 0 nominal residual degrees of freedom. This does "
"not guarantee exact fit: the model also imposes constraints on the observed "
"probabilities. G-squared and Chi-squared are descriptive discrepancies, "
"without a chi-squared reference test. Small discrepancies do not verify "
"conditional independence or parameter identifiability."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"With two categories at least %1$d cases are needed for the latent model; "
"this study has %2$d."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"With two categories at least %1$d pathologists are needed for the grading "
"tendency; this study has %2$d. With fewer, its behavior was not checked in "
"simulation."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"With two categories the latent model is not computed: in simulation its "
"estimates were biased at every design tested, whatever the number of cases "
"or pathologists, because the approximation used to fit it underestimates "
"agreement for binary calls. The headline kappa, the per-pathologist tables "
"and the boundary table remain valid."
msgstr ""

#: R/agreement.b.R
msgid ""
"With two categories the latent model needs a median of at least %1$s ratings "
"per case, because its approximation is biased with fewer ratings per case "
"whatever the number of cases; this study has %2$s."
msgstr ""

#: R/kappaSizePower.b.R
msgid ""
"With two raters, estimate the prevalence for the test by maximum likelihood "
"with kappa held at kappa0 rather than plugging in the overall proportion of "
"positive ratings, as the published procedure does: in simulations of a rare "
"finding (1 to 5 cases in 100) at a 0.05 level, the plug-in test rejected a "
"true kappa0 at a rate of 0.055-0.066 and the maximum-likelihood version at "
"0.043-0.052."
msgstr ""

#: R/nogoldstandard.b.R
msgid ""
"With two tests, a strict majority requires both tests to be positive; a "
"one-positive tie is rule negative. This is identical to the "
"all-tests-positive rule, so the positive-response probability and NPV are "
"fixed at 100% by construction and are left blank. Add a third test for a "
"non-degenerate majority rule; all values from a self-built rule describe "
"agreement, not diagnostic accuracy."
msgstr ""

